WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.270 --> 00:00:02.460
Good afternoon.

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.870
Can everyone hear me fine?

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:11.950
Welcome to Aerocene and the
Future in a Fossil-Free World.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:16.630
I'm thrilled to be moderating
and making introductions,

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:20.020
guiding the conversation
this evening

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.050
with an extraordinary
group of people.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:27.400
And we are welcoming Tomás
Saraceno here at MIT.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.340
The event is co-sponsored by
the MIT Center for Art, Science

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:34.780
&amp; Technology and the MIT
Department of Architecture.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:36.190
My name is John Fernández.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:37.720
I'm a professor
in the Department

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:40.995
of Architecture in the
Building Technology Program.

00:00:40.995 --> 00:00:43.120
And I'm the director of
the Environmental Solutions

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.380
Initiative, MIT'S
institute-wide effort

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.860
to mobilize the capacity
of our researchers,

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:54.700
our students, our community
to offer solutions

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:58.480
on climate change and other
environmental challenges

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.010
that we face in this century.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.420
It's a wonderful group.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.760
It's a highly
distinguished group.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:12.970
It's a very
multidisciplinary group.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:14.740
And because they are
so distinguished,

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:16.990
I have long
introductions for you.

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.580
So bear with me.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.160
It's worth going through them.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.860
And I will go through
them in detail

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:27.220
because I do think that this is
the best way to set up what has

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:29.080
really motivated this event.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.710
And that is to bring
disciplines together

00:01:32.710 --> 00:01:36.670
that are quite disparate and
often distant from one another

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.610
on our greatest
societal challenges

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:42.340
as one of the pathways towards
solving those challenges,

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:44.830
really addressing
those challenges.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:51.120
So beginning with Tomás
Saraceno right here, Tomás,

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.810
his work could be seen as
ongoing research informed

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.810
by worlds of art, architecture,
natural sciences, astrophysics,

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.810
and engineering and material
science, and other things,

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:02.070
actually.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:05.370
His floating sculptures and
interactive installations

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:08.729
propose and explore new
sustainable ways of inhabiting

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:10.840
and sensing the environment.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:13.530
Aerocene, an open-source
community project

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.900
for artistic and
scientific exploration

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.480
initiated from
Saraceno's vision,

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.140
becomes buoyant only
by the heat of the sun

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:26.010
and infrared radiation from
the surface of the Earth.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:28.590
In 2015, Saraceno
achieved the world record

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:31.170
for the first and longest
certified fully solar man

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:32.460
flight.

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:36.480
During the past decade, he
has initiated collaborations

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.380
with renowned
scientific institutions,

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:41.100
including MIT, the
Max Planck Institute,

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:45.000
the Nanyang Technological
University of Singapore,

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.780
and the Natural History
Museum in London.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:50.000
He was the first person
to scan, reconstruct,

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.900
and reimagine spiders' woven
spatial habitats and possesses

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:56.240
the only three-dimensional
spider web collection

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:57.000
in existence.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.320
I gather you were chasing
spiders this afternoon.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:02.710
Saraceno lectures in
institutions worldwide

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:05.830
and directed the Institute
of Architecture-related Art

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:09.440
at Braunschweig University
of Technology in Germany

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.580
and has held residencies
at Centre National d'Etudes

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.820
Spatiales, MIT's Center for
Art, Science &amp; Technology,

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:19.950
and the Atelier
Calder, among others.

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.880
I'm going to go through
all the introductions.

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:22.620
OK.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:27.120
And then the sequence will
be that they will be giving

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:28.650
10-minute--

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:30.180
10-minute-- presentations.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.200
And then we'll have
a panel discussion.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.770
Joining us is Lodovica
Illari and, really,

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:41.560
the host for Tomás today, is
a senior lecturer in the MIT

00:03:41.560 --> 00:03:44.110
Department of Earth, Atmospheric
and Planetary Sciences.

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:46.630
She earned a PhD in
atmospheric sciences

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:49.750
at Imperial College
London in 1982.

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:53.470
She teaches large-scale dynamics
and synoptic meteorology

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:56.080
in MIT'S undergraduate
and graduate programs.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:00.280
She's responsible for
the Synoptic Laboratory.

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:02.950
Illari's research interests
are in synoptic meteorology,

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:05.242
severe weather, and
atmospheric blocking.

00:04:05.242 --> 00:04:07.450
She's also involved in
developing innovative teaching

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:11.620
methods that combine fluid
laboratories with synoptic data

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:14.080
and outreach to the
public and schools,

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.510
therefore the
connection to Aerocene.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:19.450
Recently, she has collaborated
with MIT visiting artist Tomás

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:22.360
Saraceno on the Aerocene project
imagining how solar infrared

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:25.090
balloons can be used to teach
about Earth climate and sample

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:27.460
atmospheric constituents.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:29.470
We also have--
thrilled to have Dan.

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:32.860
Welcome, Dan Cziczo, also
from the MIT Department

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:36.160
of Earth, Atmospheric
and Planetary Sciences.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.910
He's professor of atmospheric
chemistry since 2011.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:42.220
And his research
focuses on the role

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:45.010
that particulate matter plays
in atmospheric chemistry,

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:46.690
visibility, and
cloud formations.

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:48.610
Visiting his lab is
quite an experience.

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:53.470
He creates very small
atmospheres in his machines,

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:54.880
actually.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:58.060
Examples include understanding
how aerosols nucleate water

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:00.490
and ice clouds, improving
the quantification

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:05.740
of meteoritic ablation products
and determining the abundance

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:09.180
of aircraft and rocket exhaust.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.870
Cziczo's undergraduate work
was in aerospace engineering.

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:14.270
And he spent two years at
the Jet Propulsion Laboratory

00:05:14.270 --> 00:05:17.120
performing spacecraft
navigation before starting

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:18.560
his graduate work.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.220
Cziczo received an MS and
PhD in geophysical sciences

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:25.730
from the University of Chicago
for work with Professor John

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:26.930
Abbott.

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:30.590
His postdoctoral fellowship
was with Dr. Daniel Murphy

00:05:30.590 --> 00:05:33.920
at NOAA [INAUDIBLE] Laboratory,
and he spent three years

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.840
at the Swiss Federal
Institute of Technology

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:38.390
working with Professor
Ulrike Lohmann.

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:41.360
Before coming to MIT, Cziczo
was a research scientist

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.120
at the Pacific Northwest
National Laboratory,

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.460
where he directed their
atmospheric measurement

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:49.480
laboratory.

00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:55.430
And finally, Bob Jaffe is
the Jane and Otto Morningstar

00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:57.620
professor of physics at MIT.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:05:59.330
A theoretical
physicist, his research

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:01.790
focuses on the physics
of elementary particles

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:04.310
and quantum field theory,
especially the dynamics

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:06.860
of quark confinement
and the standard model.

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:09.830
He has served as an advisor to
several national laboratories

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:12.260
and universities, including
as chair of the Science

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:14.510
and Engineering Steering
Committee of the Brookhaven

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:15.830
National Laboratory.

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:17.690
At MIT, Jaffe has
served as chair

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:20.420
of the faculty and
director of the Center

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:22.430
for Theoretical Physics
and has received

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:25.630
many awards for teaching
and course development.

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:31.960
From 2008 to 2016,
Jaffe was a member

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:33.670
of the American
Physical Society's panel

00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:38.010
on public affairs, which
he chaired in 2015.

00:06:38.010 --> 00:06:42.540
In 2011, Jaffe led a joint
American Physical Society panel

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:46.980
on public affairs and MRS study
on energy-critical elements

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:50.340
securing materials for
emerging technologies.

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.380
And together with Professor
Washington Taylor,

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:54.570
Jaffe developed and
taught a new course

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:56.490
on the physics of
energy, which was

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:59.490
adopted as a foundational
requirement for MIT'S energy

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:01.370
minor degree.

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:04.550
The textbook, which we all
waited with bated breath

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:07.220
to come out and has been
out since March, correct?

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:09.350
Mm-hmm.

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:14.390
--is an outgrowth of the
course, the Physics of Energy,

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:16.130
and was published
this past March.

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:18.560
Jaffe is a fellow of the
American Academy of Arts

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:20.510
and Sciences, the
American Association

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:23.060
for the Advancement of Science,
and the American Physical

00:07:23.060 --> 00:07:24.230
Society.

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:25.730
So welcome to all.

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:29.340
I'd like to have Tomás come up
and give us your presentation.

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:31.073
Thank you.

00:07:31.073 --> 00:07:34.510
[APPLAUSE]

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:39.080
Yeah, hello?

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:39.660
Hello?

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:40.230
Yes.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:41.003
Hello?

00:07:41.003 --> 00:07:41.670
Can you hear me?

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:42.870
Yes, wonderful.

00:07:42.870 --> 00:07:45.660
Maybe we can turn a
little bit down the light

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:46.865
if it's possible.

00:07:46.865 --> 00:07:48.120
[INAUDIBLE]

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:48.620
Sorry?

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:50.240
[INAUDIBLE]

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:50.740
Video.

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:55.850
LLB.

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:56.720
50%.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:58.190
Thank you.

00:07:58.190 --> 00:08:02.490
I'm a visual artist
sometimes, and I love it.

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:03.797
We try to focus on the images.

00:08:03.797 --> 00:08:05.630
And the other thing is
that I promised today

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:09.170
that I would really try to
keep it within 10 minutes.

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:11.800
And maybe you kind of
held me to the nine.

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:14.750
Oh, you got it.

00:08:14.750 --> 00:08:15.500
What should I say?

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:16.610
It is a honor.

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:20.210
And I'm very, very happy
to be back at my team.

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:22.880
I feel a little bit
being at home sometimes.

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:25.200
And they say, oh,
when we come today.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.790
I'm again with good friends.

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:30.230
And this means I'm very,
very happy to be here back.

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:32.840
And there are things
a little bit--

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:34.640
what is happening
with the project is,

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:38.809
somehow, it became so
much weather dependent.

00:08:38.809 --> 00:08:41.900
So we have been so much
talking during the last days

00:08:41.900 --> 00:08:44.330
that it's cloudy.

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:46.400
And hopefully the
sun will come out

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:48.950
to be able to perform
some activities.

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:53.210
Down the road, there is
engagement psychologically

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:57.590
that somehow I became much
more aware of the weather

00:08:57.590 --> 00:08:58.220
down the road.

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:02.390
Now, I have a presentation.

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:07.430
I will really try to compress
it and go as fast as possible

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:09.380
and try to match
it into 10 minutes.

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:14.548
There we go.

00:09:14.548 --> 00:09:16.340
This is one of my
favorite places on Earth,

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:19.100
the Salar de Uyuni in Bolivia.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.170
When it's covered with
a thin layer of water,

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:22.520
the clouds reflect.

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:25.220
And there are days
one feels as if you

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:28.340
were floating in the clouds.

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:30.540
But there are days when
the horizon disappears.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:33.500
And there is never
either above nor below.

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:35.160
You feel immersed
in something bigger.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:39.650
Where does the sky end
and the Earth begin?

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:44.910
And it was there that one
night, in the middle of a dream,

00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:45.540
I woke up.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:49.310
And I saw that the stars
also reflected on the water.

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:50.330
And it was not a dream.

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:53.290
It was like I could
walk among the stars.

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:56.530
Every step I took, the stars
reverberated under my feet.

00:09:56.530 --> 00:09:59.260
And it was like feeling
floating in the universe

00:09:59.260 --> 00:10:01.960
among the clouds of galaxies.

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:03.527
I do not know about
you, but I always

00:10:03.527 --> 00:10:05.110
dream about floating
among the clouds.

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:08.080
And today, maybe we can
imagine this together.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:10.870
We can live in an ecosystem
floating among the clouds.

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:13.900
Cloud cities is a
long-term project of mine

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:17.650
where we speculate and venture
of new urban dwellings,

00:10:17.650 --> 00:10:19.630
nomadic and transnational.

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:25.190
They belong to everybody.

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:29.270
It does not depend on any
type of [INAUDIBLE] in the way

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:31.280
that we hope that cloud
cities could anticipate

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:37.420
a borderless, open-source,
free-flying utopian existence.

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:42.280
And as we said
before, imagine is

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:45.462
the force that can lead
us to create new places.

00:10:45.462 --> 00:10:47.920
This is an installation I did
on a roof of the Metropolitan

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:49.640
Museum in New York
in which we speculate

00:10:49.640 --> 00:10:52.750
how this transnational
space could be.

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:55.510
The image of the
Earth from space

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:58.840
does not show any borders,
any special constraint.

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:04.270
But on it, we build vertical
and horizontal boundaries.

00:11:04.270 --> 00:11:06.520
This image, for example,
shows that airspace

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:08.170
is divided into
sections, allowing

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:11.380
different topology of
fossil fuel aircraft

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:16.480
to each one of them and
project national borders

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:17.830
into the atmosphere.

00:11:17.830 --> 00:11:19.690
It causes a nightmare
every time we

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:23.140
launch one of these sculptures
to cross their airspace.

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:24.640
This is why I think
we could not let

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:30.120
it fly from here because there
are too many airports nearby.

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:34.130
While enterprises to colonize
other planets are put in place,

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:36.010
this very same
interface between us

00:11:36.010 --> 00:11:39.010
and the sun and the atmosphere
continues to be compromised.

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:40.630
Carbon emissions fill the air.

00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:43.840
Invisible radio waves develop
in a hegemonic algorithm

00:11:43.840 --> 00:11:45.520
of dominance.

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:48.580
Particulate matter
floats inside our lungs.

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:50.980
Air has been colonized
by drones, satellites,

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:52.930
and military devices
and strictly regulated

00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:55.030
by national policies and land.

00:11:55.030 --> 00:12:00.560
How could breathing feel in
a post-fossil fuel economy?

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:03.130
It is urgent to rethink
how we can coexist

00:12:03.130 --> 00:12:05.890
with a planet and its
resources and what

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:08.590
a stratigraphy of the
future will look like.

00:12:08.590 --> 00:12:10.600
Yet, what would float
today in the water

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:13.250
are not only cloud of galaxies
but also cloud of plastics.

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:16.360
These are the traces that we
are leaving on the planet,

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:18.670
are the marks of
the era in which

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:23.100
the behavior of some people
is leaving a global impact

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:24.580
on the rest of the ecosystem.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:26.500
This time has been
named the Anthropocene.

00:12:33.278 --> 00:12:34.820
All of these have
transformed the way

00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:37.400
human senses react to the air.

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:39.350
To achieve long-lasting
change, people

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:41.660
must relearn the relationship
with the elements

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:45.170
and understand, how can we
live in tandem with them

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:47.120
rather than control it?

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:51.020
To be aware of the air is to
sense it to one's environment.

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:55.820
To be aware of the element which
sustain you, transmit, carry.

00:12:55.820 --> 00:13:00.690
If you leave a room, vacate
a building, climb a mountain,

00:13:00.690 --> 00:13:03.320
float in the sea, you are
still contained within the air.

00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:06.020
The air senses the environment.

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:09.440
But there is also any kind
of social structure thing.

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:10.610
So we kind of run late.

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:11.570
We got five minutes.

00:13:15.637 --> 00:13:17.970
A little bit to understand
the beginning of the project,

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:19.520
we start to collect a
lot of plastic bags.

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:20.120
We wash them.

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:20.780
We take care.

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:22.460
We cut them.

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:24.030
We paste them.

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:26.270
And we begin to draw on them.

00:13:26.270 --> 00:13:28.080
Creating gigantic
canvases, we create

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.455
a collection of growing personal
stories and friendships.

00:13:30.455 --> 00:13:32.360
[CHEER]

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:37.120
And when the canvas unite
fold bends, a space is formed.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:41.890
Once full of air, it's
not a matter of time

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:44.800
until the air inside the
sculpture in the sun coming up

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:48.400
from the horizon, the sculpture
rises up into the air.

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:52.180
As simple as it looks.

00:13:52.180 --> 00:13:55.630
The way how it floats is
really, really simple.

00:13:55.630 --> 00:13:57.220
As such, that is
so simple, there

00:13:57.220 --> 00:14:02.500
is also no plant or animal
who floats in the same way

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:04.520
how this sculpture floats.

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:07.900
There's no plant or animal
in the entire planet

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:10.550
who floats by this
differential of temperature.

00:14:10.550 --> 00:14:15.130
There are many pollen, bacterias
who are raised in the thermics.

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:16.705
And they might get
up into the air

00:14:16.705 --> 00:14:18.580
but not by these
differential of temperature.

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:20.140
It's the same as a cumulus.

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:23.440
When you float in the water,
you put your lungs full of air.

00:14:23.440 --> 00:14:26.500
And then somehow, you float
in the water in the same way

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:28.570
these sculptures
get up into the air.

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:30.940
And in this way, we
don't use fossil fuel

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:34.090
like a normal balloon might
have a burner or a candle.

00:14:34.090 --> 00:14:36.370
And then many other
people are also

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:38.530
heating the air through
solar panel batteries

00:14:38.530 --> 00:14:41.050
and then using also,
many times, helium,

00:14:41.050 --> 00:14:44.440
which is a byproduct many times
of fossil fuel instructions.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:50.208
And hydrogen is quite
complicated, also, to make it.

00:14:50.208 --> 00:14:52.000
Following on that, we
thought, oh, maybe we

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:53.980
could think about
a new epoch who

00:14:53.980 --> 00:14:57.190
might get us more
proactive in that extent

00:14:57.190 --> 00:14:59.800
and think about different
metabolic regime of how humans

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:01.840
also have inhabited the Earth.

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:04.270
And this might be the
fourth metabolic regime,

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:06.310
to a certain extent.

00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:08.863
And this is why these
is the backpack.

00:15:08.863 --> 00:15:10.030
And these are the backpacks.

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:11.530
There is this sculpture.

00:15:11.530 --> 00:15:14.350
And this is the one that
we will try tomorrow

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:18.730
on Friday and on Saturday
to get up into the air.

00:15:18.730 --> 00:15:20.200
They have different
devices, which

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:22.780
I think is what, with
Lodo and many communities

00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:25.450
around the world, we are
trying to optimize and to think

00:15:25.450 --> 00:15:27.940
of how we could sense
the air differently.

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:29.740
In this case, there's
a motion tracker.

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:31.630
And then make these
beautiful drawings.

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:33.630
There are different
communities around the world

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:36.430
which are really hacking,
transforming, and changing.

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:39.400
This means I'm
very happy to hear

00:15:39.400 --> 00:15:43.000
what will be the devices
we will lift up tomorrow.

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:45.580
Here, there's a
performance that we

00:15:45.580 --> 00:15:47.380
are trying to put up together.

00:15:47.380 --> 00:15:51.820
There is Joaquin, which is
coming from Argentina, also.

00:15:51.820 --> 00:15:54.400
And he's working
with a community.

00:15:54.400 --> 00:15:56.350
You want to tell
us two words only?

00:15:56.350 --> 00:15:56.990
Two words?

00:15:56.990 --> 00:15:57.490
Yes.

00:15:57.490 --> 00:16:00.280
Very fast.

00:16:00.280 --> 00:16:01.930
Yeah.

00:16:01.930 --> 00:16:02.500
Hi there.

00:16:02.500 --> 00:16:03.230
My pleasure.

00:16:03.230 --> 00:16:05.890
I'm just going to show
you brief images of how

00:16:05.890 --> 00:16:09.430
we're building a community
around the Aerocene project

00:16:09.430 --> 00:16:10.670
in Argentina.

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:12.370
This is how we brainstorm.

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:16.060
We get together for [INAUDIBLE]
and we discussed-- or barbecue.

00:16:16.060 --> 00:16:18.550
We discussed some ideas with
Tomás and a group of people

00:16:18.550 --> 00:16:20.530
in Buenos Aires
back in December.

00:16:20.530 --> 00:16:24.700
And I'd like to just tell
you about this woman, who's

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:28.240
called Debora, and wrote
a book called Flammable

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:33.100
about this community living
near a petrochemical plant.

00:16:33.100 --> 00:16:37.660
And basically, it's very
near the center of the city.

00:16:37.660 --> 00:16:41.470
And they have huge
health problems.

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:45.100
And so we have
partnered with people

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:48.880
to create an air
quality monitor.

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:51.130
And we have been
talking with MIT people

00:16:51.130 --> 00:16:56.650
today to see how we can improve
this and basically get the air

00:16:56.650 --> 00:16:58.330
quality monitor airborne.

00:16:58.330 --> 00:17:04.480
And also, we are thinking about
ideas of having an aerial mesh

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:09.369
network that is 100% solar
powered, the first ever

00:17:09.369 --> 00:17:12.680
solar-powered
aerial mesh network.

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:14.829
And over to Tomás.

00:17:14.829 --> 00:17:16.599
Yes, I think we have one minute.

00:17:16.599 --> 00:17:17.440
Or zero.

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:18.910
I can stop whenever you want.

00:17:18.910 --> 00:17:20.950
I don't have any pressure.

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:23.499
But yeah, there is a lot
of life up into the air.

00:17:23.499 --> 00:17:25.124
While some of the
sculptures, yes, they

00:17:25.124 --> 00:17:28.119
have been free flying, we
launched some from Berlin

00:17:28.119 --> 00:17:29.290
most of the time.

00:17:29.290 --> 00:17:32.680
We get a lot of problem with
getting the air regulation

00:17:32.680 --> 00:17:35.620
being able to tune with
these type of sculptures.

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:37.840
We will be next week talking
with a European minister

00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:40.540
of transportation trying
to change the rite of pass

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:41.945
for these type of vehicles.

00:17:41.945 --> 00:17:43.570
This means also we
are trying to set up

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:45.195
a different legal
framework in relation

00:17:45.195 --> 00:17:48.160
with insurance and
translation of papers.

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:51.598
And we will try to lift up also
the minister up into the air.

00:17:51.598 --> 00:17:53.140
This means some of
the things that we

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:55.270
have been doing with
Lodo, and I think

00:17:55.270 --> 00:17:57.660
she will show the floating
predictor, and Glenn

00:17:57.660 --> 00:17:59.710
and Bill and EAPS.

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:02.323
It's this idea of really
being able to circumnavigate

00:18:02.323 --> 00:18:04.240
the Earth also during
the night and picking up

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:06.862
the infraradiation from
the air, something which

00:18:06.862 --> 00:18:08.320
CNES, the French
Space Agency, have

00:18:08.320 --> 00:18:09.640
been doing during the '70s.

00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:12.340
And somehow, the program
had been forgotten.

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:18.730
Yeah, OK.

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:20.730
These are some of the
sculptures that we present

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:24.420
you COP21 conference in Paris
at the Grand Palais, which

00:18:24.420 --> 00:18:26.160
are the ones that
more or less will

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:31.290
be able to fly also during
the night and ride the wind.

00:18:31.290 --> 00:18:33.390
So a lot of these,
you will talk about.

00:18:33.390 --> 00:18:35.360
This means I skip that slide.

00:18:35.360 --> 00:18:37.650
But what we want is
kind of artistic, also.

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:42.060
It's like all these drawings,
feather flight, free fly,

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:44.550
or imaginary flights.

00:18:44.550 --> 00:18:46.140
This collection,
somehow, it will

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:49.650
become kind of a signature
towards maybe an Independence

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:52.550
Day of fossil fuel that
we are all trying to sign.

00:18:52.550 --> 00:18:54.480
We were collecting
all these drawings,

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:58.530
and we are lifting up different
people up into the air, also.

00:18:58.530 --> 00:19:02.040
And this was a performance which
was done in the White Sands

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:05.340
in New Mexico, where it
was kind of the beginning

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:08.010
of the Anthropocene
with the first atomic--

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:08.610
stop.

00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:09.750
OK, I stop.

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:10.770
I promised 10 minutes.

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:11.957
Thank you.

00:19:11.957 --> 00:19:15.436
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:23.885 --> 00:19:25.000
We did the connection.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:25.728
Could I?

00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:51.550
Hi.

00:19:51.550 --> 00:19:57.100
It's a great pleasure to be
here and also a big task to me

00:19:57.100 --> 00:19:59.110
to follow Tomás's step.

00:19:59.110 --> 00:20:02.530
But what I'm going to do is
talk a bit about the science

00:20:02.530 --> 00:20:04.540
we did in the Aerocene project.

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:07.750
And the work I'm going to
describe is not only my work.

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:12.370
It's in very close collaboration
with Professor Glenn Flierl

00:20:12.370 --> 00:20:16.090
and Bill McKenna, both in EAPS.

00:20:16.090 --> 00:20:19.630
And the collaboration with Tomás
started quite a few years ago.

00:20:19.630 --> 00:20:24.790
And I'm mainly focusing
on describing some work

00:20:24.790 --> 00:20:27.400
on exploring the
use of solar balloon

00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:29.740
as a platform for
observing the atmosphere.

00:20:29.740 --> 00:20:32.740
Tomás has already
mentioned that in his talk.

00:20:32.740 --> 00:20:34.420
On the side here--

00:20:34.420 --> 00:20:35.800
sorry, I want to go back.

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:37.930
But you are just
seeing the movie of one

00:20:37.930 --> 00:20:39.670
of these solar balloons.

00:20:39.670 --> 00:20:43.510
And this is the one we try
to fly again this weekend.

00:20:43.510 --> 00:20:47.420
And that describes a little bit
more what we want to do next.

00:20:47.420 --> 00:20:50.850
So the stages of
our collaboration,

00:20:50.850 --> 00:20:53.710
I split it in three--

00:20:53.710 --> 00:20:59.020
some study of historical
balloons from the French Space

00:20:59.020 --> 00:21:03.940
Agency that we presented
in Paris at COP21.

00:21:03.940 --> 00:21:07.900
Then some work on virtual
balloon flights with the float

00:21:07.900 --> 00:21:10.240
predictor that Tomás
had just mentioned.

00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:17.720
And then some tethered
balloon, the explorer

00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:22.030
that we want to use for chemical
sampling of the atmosphere

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:25.590
and particles on campus.

00:21:25.590 --> 00:21:32.330
So this is the French
attempt, a very successful use

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:34.220
of solar balloons.

00:21:34.220 --> 00:21:37.040
They're actually called
Montgolfiere Infrarouge.

00:21:37.040 --> 00:21:39.320
They're solar and infrared.

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:40.820
And this is one of the balloons.

00:21:40.820 --> 00:21:41.960
It's a huge one.

00:21:41.960 --> 00:21:44.420
It's intended to reach
the stratosphere.

00:21:44.420 --> 00:21:46.760
And it's filled with just air.

00:21:46.760 --> 00:21:50.030
And it's got a
particular configuration.

00:21:50.030 --> 00:21:52.670
If you look in here
at the diagram,

00:21:52.670 --> 00:21:58.070
you can see the top is a
polyester cover with aluminum.

00:21:58.070 --> 00:22:02.930
So if the sun shines on it,
the aluminum gets very hot,

00:22:02.930 --> 00:22:05.810
heats the air
inside the balloon.

00:22:05.810 --> 00:22:07.303
And it gets expanded.

00:22:07.303 --> 00:22:07.970
The air expands.

00:22:07.970 --> 00:22:09.740
It becomes buoyant.

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:11.390
But there is a
particular feature

00:22:11.390 --> 00:22:14.840
that I want to point out
on the bottom of the-- it's

00:22:14.840 --> 00:22:15.740
transparent.

00:22:15.740 --> 00:22:18.290
If you notice here, it's
actually transparent.

00:22:18.290 --> 00:22:20.180
And it's deliberately
done because he

00:22:20.180 --> 00:22:23.870
wants to harness also the
infrared radiation from hertz.

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:27.020
So you know that the Earth
is a body which emits

00:22:27.020 --> 00:22:28.500
in all directions infrared.

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:30.320
And so some of these
infrared radiation

00:22:30.320 --> 00:22:32.120
would be captured
by the balloon.

00:22:32.120 --> 00:22:34.160
So imagine the sun goes down.

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:36.080
The air inside gets colder.

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:40.100
But enough infrared radiation
is captured to keep it buoyant.

00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:43.040
And this is the name,
Montgolfiere Infrarouge.

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:46.560
And here is data that we
acquired through the airport

00:22:46.560 --> 00:22:47.420
Tomás--

00:22:47.420 --> 00:22:50.750
from his internship
at CNES, real data

00:22:50.750 --> 00:22:56.840
of one of these flights launched
in Brazil in February, 2004.

00:22:56.840 --> 00:23:01.460
It reached 25 kilometers
high in the stratosphere.

00:23:01.460 --> 00:23:06.310
And it then got colder because
the night came, came down,

00:23:06.310 --> 00:23:09.560
then up again, and then down
again the following night.

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:14.660
And then it adjusted to a kind
of fluctuating day and night

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:19.110
undulation from between
25 and 20 kilometers.

00:23:19.110 --> 00:23:22.400
And it flew like
that for 12 days.

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:29.120
This is a 12-days track,
very smoothly steady on wind

00:23:29.120 --> 00:23:30.650
from east to west.

00:23:30.650 --> 00:23:32.900
And just to describe
the structure

00:23:32.900 --> 00:23:36.290
of the wind in the atmosphere,
we made a section here.

00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:40.610
And the blue and cyan, we have
wind blowing from the west.

00:23:40.610 --> 00:23:42.920
And in the pink
and gold, we have

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:44.750
wind blowing from the east.

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:48.230
So clearly, at this
level in the stratosphere

00:23:48.230 --> 00:23:52.280
at this particular latitude,
you have a very strong flow

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:53.120
from the east.

00:23:53.120 --> 00:24:00.020
And this is what was used
to make the balloon travel.

00:24:00.020 --> 00:24:04.490
The idea, the balloon was
intended to measure chemicals--

00:24:04.490 --> 00:24:08.760
ozone, methane, water
vapor, all chemicals

00:24:08.760 --> 00:24:12.380
very sensitive to
the climate of Earth.

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:16.190
And I just want to mention
that many flights were

00:24:16.190 --> 00:24:17.810
done by the CNES.

00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:21.440
And there's a record of 72 days.

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:26.120
So they kept it up for 72
days is the record in 2001.

00:24:26.120 --> 00:24:31.340
And it was very
successful in the tropics.

00:24:31.340 --> 00:24:33.200
They had more
problem in the Arctic

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:37.040
because you can imagine that if
you have clouds at low level,

00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:38.930
then they stop the
infrared radiation.

00:24:38.930 --> 00:24:40.460
And the balloon will come up.

00:24:40.460 --> 00:24:43.400
So very successful for
the tropical exploration

00:24:43.400 --> 00:24:47.210
of the stratosphere, but not
so much for the extratropic

00:24:47.210 --> 00:24:48.830
in the Arctic.

00:24:48.830 --> 00:24:52.850
So Saraceno, after
his internship,

00:24:52.850 --> 00:24:54.800
came to visit Azir at MIT.

00:24:54.800 --> 00:24:59.900
He wanted to repropose
this technology.

00:24:59.900 --> 00:25:04.040
And we made a study of the data.

00:25:04.040 --> 00:25:06.710
And we presented
the work at COP21.

00:25:06.710 --> 00:25:09.590
And I think this was the
beginning of our collaboration.

00:25:09.590 --> 00:25:11.570
And I actually was
hooked on this data.

00:25:11.570 --> 00:25:13.280
It was amazing
for me to discover

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:16.220
that you could keep a
balloon of that dimension

00:25:16.220 --> 00:25:19.630
for so long flying
in the stratosphere.

00:25:19.630 --> 00:25:23.140
Now, the second part
of our collaboration

00:25:23.140 --> 00:25:26.530
is instead involving
and saying, well,

00:25:26.530 --> 00:25:28.810
this Aerocene sculpture go--

00:25:28.810 --> 00:25:32.620
I want to mention here Tomás
doesn't call them balloons

00:25:32.620 --> 00:25:34.180
anymore because
they're beautiful.

00:25:34.180 --> 00:25:37.390
They're really much
better than the other one

00:25:37.390 --> 00:25:38.350
I've just shown you.

00:25:38.350 --> 00:25:42.050
These are his sculptures
presented at COP21 in Paris.

00:25:42.050 --> 00:25:43.957
And so the question
that he has is where

00:25:43.957 --> 00:25:45.040
these sculptures could go.

00:25:45.040 --> 00:25:47.950
Can I go up on one level,
come down on the other,

00:25:47.950 --> 00:25:51.550
take the westerly, the
easterly, go around in a circle?

00:25:51.550 --> 00:25:54.790
And so we developed together
with Professor Glenn Flierl

00:25:54.790 --> 00:25:58.060
and Bill an
interactive interface

00:25:58.060 --> 00:26:03.310
to be able to check where
this virtual balloon could go.

00:26:03.310 --> 00:26:07.210
So I want to give you a
quick demonstration of this.

00:26:07.210 --> 00:26:09.190
And I'm going out now.

00:26:09.190 --> 00:26:13.180
I'm going to be
kind of adventurous,

00:26:13.180 --> 00:26:14.840
going out on the web.

00:26:14.840 --> 00:26:17.050
And this is something that
is going to be available

00:26:17.050 --> 00:26:19.450
for you to explore
after the talk

00:26:19.450 --> 00:26:22.030
and also tomorrow in Lobby 10.

00:26:22.030 --> 00:26:23.350
And it's plotted here.

00:26:23.350 --> 00:26:25.300
It's an interactive interface.

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:27.730
We can actually move
the sphere and check.

00:26:27.730 --> 00:26:29.950
This is the wind at
10 kilometers high.

00:26:29.950 --> 00:26:33.790
The wind speed coded in
color from weak to strong

00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:36.280
is the pink is very strong.

00:26:36.280 --> 00:26:38.860
And you see the
traditional, common feature

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:40.420
of jet in the atmosphere.

00:26:40.420 --> 00:26:43.360
We are all familiar with the
jet stream covering the weather

00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:44.090
system.

00:26:44.090 --> 00:26:49.700
And this is actually
driven by today's data.

00:26:49.700 --> 00:26:51.670
This is the forecast
data that is

00:26:51.670 --> 00:26:53.920
used for the 10-day forecast.

00:26:53.920 --> 00:26:57.130
It's actually 16-day forecast
from the National Weather

00:26:57.130 --> 00:26:58.270
Service.

00:26:58.270 --> 00:27:03.970
So I want to give you
just a small example

00:27:03.970 --> 00:27:05.680
of what you could do.

00:27:05.680 --> 00:27:08.050
So imagine I'm saying
I start from Boston,

00:27:08.050 --> 00:27:11.500
and I'm now at 10 kilometers
high with this wind.

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:12.830
Where should I go?

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:14.110
So I click here.

00:27:14.110 --> 00:27:17.950
And immediately, you have
an interactive trajectory

00:27:17.950 --> 00:27:20.120
computing following the flow.

00:27:20.120 --> 00:27:21.280
So this, you're following.

00:27:21.280 --> 00:27:22.660
So the flow is evolving.

00:27:22.660 --> 00:27:26.020
And you're moving with the
flow, like the balloon of Tomás.

00:27:26.020 --> 00:27:29.750
And you can see that it reached
Europe and the Mediterranean.

00:27:29.750 --> 00:27:31.162
It's got diverted quite a bit.

00:27:31.162 --> 00:27:32.620
And then it's come
back in the jet.

00:27:32.620 --> 00:27:35.260
But it's not managed
to go around the globe.

00:27:35.260 --> 00:27:37.840
Now, the wind is very
different at different level.

00:27:37.840 --> 00:27:40.750
If you scroll through, you
can see the difference.

00:27:40.750 --> 00:27:44.350
Much weaker at the surface,
stronger at the jet level,

00:27:44.350 --> 00:27:47.470
then strong again
but weaker at Europe.

00:27:47.470 --> 00:27:49.630
And I'm choosing
the 25 kilometer

00:27:49.630 --> 00:27:53.410
because it's where the
Montgolfiere Infrarouge used

00:27:53.410 --> 00:27:54.340
to fly.

00:27:54.340 --> 00:27:57.370
And now I'm trying to do the
same experiment that they

00:27:57.370 --> 00:27:59.320
were used to do with this wind.

00:27:59.320 --> 00:28:02.650
I'm put in a balloon here
and see where it will go.

00:28:02.650 --> 00:28:05.380
And you can see they were
pretty clever, the French.

00:28:05.380 --> 00:28:08.440
They knew there was this
band of easterly flow.

00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:09.730
And they could follow.

00:28:09.730 --> 00:28:13.630
And if I show you the
change of this trajectory,

00:28:13.630 --> 00:28:17.110
I think it is going to go around
all the globe in approximately

00:28:17.110 --> 00:28:18.700
15 days.

00:28:18.700 --> 00:28:25.780
And exploring this interface
has been good not only

00:28:25.780 --> 00:28:28.300
for exploring the wind
and the atmosphere

00:28:28.300 --> 00:28:30.910
but also teaching the main
feature of the atmosphere

00:28:30.910 --> 00:28:31.660
in my classes.

00:28:31.660 --> 00:28:34.000
So it's been a great
development from the point

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:36.230
of view of our teaching.

00:28:36.230 --> 00:28:38.650
So I'm going to go back
to the presentation

00:28:38.650 --> 00:28:41.800
and show you the
third stage, which

00:28:41.800 --> 00:28:46.450
is making use instead not free
flight, but a tethered balloon.

00:28:46.450 --> 00:28:48.880
You can imagine that
you have a free flight.

00:28:48.880 --> 00:28:51.550
You have to go through
permission from the FAA.

00:28:51.550 --> 00:28:52.780
It's not easy.

00:28:52.780 --> 00:28:56.770
And so we thought, why not try
to experiment with a smaller

00:28:56.770 --> 00:28:59.770
balloon which has tether
and try to use them

00:28:59.770 --> 00:29:04.310
to sample pollution in
the lower troposphere?

00:29:04.310 --> 00:29:07.600
So this is our first
attempt last summer in July,

00:29:07.600 --> 00:29:08.860
when it was very hot.

00:29:08.860 --> 00:29:09.630
It went up.

00:29:09.630 --> 00:29:11.440
It got buoyant very easily.

00:29:11.440 --> 00:29:12.460
It got up.

00:29:12.460 --> 00:29:15.160
It got up nearly at the
top of the Green Building.

00:29:15.160 --> 00:29:18.100
We have to keep it down because
we couldn't raise it higher

00:29:18.100 --> 00:29:19.840
than the Green Building.

00:29:19.840 --> 00:29:25.330
And then we did another
experiment in October.

00:29:25.330 --> 00:29:29.050
And tomorrow and next Saturday
will be our third attempt

00:29:29.050 --> 00:29:30.740
with different sensor.

00:29:30.740 --> 00:29:33.190
So the science
application is monitoring

00:29:33.190 --> 00:29:35.140
atmospheric chemical
and particles

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:36.790
in the lower troposphere.

00:29:36.790 --> 00:29:40.430
The collaboration this time
is with Professor Jesse Kroll

00:29:40.430 --> 00:29:43.840
and his student, which is
an expert in monitoring.

00:29:43.840 --> 00:29:46.510
But he mainly does
it at the ground.

00:29:46.510 --> 00:29:49.000
He's not done really
vertical profile.

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:52.450
So he's really excited to try
to test the vertical profile

00:29:52.450 --> 00:29:53.770
of this pollutant.

00:29:53.770 --> 00:29:56.770
Which variable are we
interested in monitoring?

00:29:56.770 --> 00:29:58.860
What can we measure, and where?

00:29:58.860 --> 00:30:01.900
Here, we try to
summarize a little bit

00:30:01.900 --> 00:30:04.660
that there are different
variables because there

00:30:04.660 --> 00:30:05.800
are different structures.

00:30:05.800 --> 00:30:08.750
If you look at NOX,
which is a big pollutant,

00:30:08.750 --> 00:30:12.130
it's really confined in
the lower troposphere.

00:30:12.130 --> 00:30:18.460
This is also zone product of
interaction with NOX, which is

00:30:18.460 --> 00:30:21.100
also very local in the lower.

00:30:21.100 --> 00:30:26.350
Carbon monoxide is also local
down in the troposphere.

00:30:26.350 --> 00:30:29.680
And then a lot of
different particles,

00:30:29.680 --> 00:30:32.470
they could be residual
from combustion.

00:30:32.470 --> 00:30:33.670
They could be dust.

00:30:33.670 --> 00:30:37.360
And they can spread as high
as 10 kilometers, even more.

00:30:37.360 --> 00:30:39.220
And Dan will talk about those.

00:30:39.220 --> 00:30:40.390
He's an expert.

00:30:40.390 --> 00:30:43.270
Ozone tends to be
concentrated higher up.

00:30:43.270 --> 00:30:45.250
And this is where those
stratospheric flights,

00:30:45.250 --> 00:30:46.292
we are trying to measure.

00:30:46.292 --> 00:30:50.500
The maximum ozone production is
actually around 20 kilometers.

00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:54.475
And the usual thermodynamic
variable that I'm interested in

00:30:54.475 --> 00:30:55.225
is in meteorology.

00:30:55.225 --> 00:30:56.360
It's temperature.

00:30:56.360 --> 00:30:59.140
Humidity will also be a target.

00:30:59.140 --> 00:31:00.650
How can we measure them?

00:31:00.650 --> 00:31:03.220
So we want to create
a community of users

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:05.980
all over the world to
measure this quantity

00:31:05.980 --> 00:31:08.290
and with the balloons.

00:31:08.290 --> 00:31:09.130
But what type?

00:31:09.130 --> 00:31:13.570
We can use the mere balloon
higher up, a suggestion to you.

00:31:13.570 --> 00:31:18.460
Imagine it hovering up and down
for 72 days or even longer,

00:31:18.460 --> 00:31:21.640
if you can, and taking
measurements of ozone, methane.

00:31:21.640 --> 00:31:25.540
Very important for
the greenhouse effect.

00:31:25.540 --> 00:31:28.900
And lower here, much easier.

00:31:28.900 --> 00:31:31.510
And we can even do it
on campus with students

00:31:31.510 --> 00:31:34.600
and measure the pollution
with this tethered balloon.

00:31:34.600 --> 00:31:39.790
So we're going to try to do this
tomorrow if the sun comes out.

00:31:39.790 --> 00:31:43.960
If it doesn't tomorrow,
forecast is telling us--

00:31:43.960 --> 00:31:45.100
not mostly.

00:31:45.100 --> 00:31:47.440
I wrote this
presentation yesterday.

00:31:47.440 --> 00:31:50.630
It's actually sunny
without the "mostly" now.

00:31:50.630 --> 00:31:53.350
So I'm very confident
Saturday will be a good day.

00:31:53.350 --> 00:31:54.430
So take your time.

00:31:54.430 --> 00:31:55.060
Come out.

00:31:55.060 --> 00:31:56.620
And come to watch us.

00:31:56.620 --> 00:32:01.465
Now, finally and just the last
thing is this is an [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:01.465 --> 00:32:08.710
for an educational activity,
I think educational,

00:32:08.710 --> 00:32:13.090
which is ongoing, which we
call it EsGlobe, Environmental

00:32:13.090 --> 00:32:14.140
science Globe.

00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:16.720
It's a project
funded by the ASI.

00:32:16.720 --> 00:32:20.350
And the idea is to
have an interactive way

00:32:20.350 --> 00:32:23.110
of displaying data, very
similar to what I've shown you

00:32:23.110 --> 00:32:24.610
for the Aerocene.

00:32:24.610 --> 00:32:26.560
But you can be any data.

00:32:26.560 --> 00:32:30.640
And you can manipulate it, plot
it in the sphere, and use it.

00:32:30.640 --> 00:32:33.460
So I just finish and
say come to join us

00:32:33.460 --> 00:32:35.500
and explore this
in the lobby here.

00:32:35.500 --> 00:32:37.660
I'm with Tomás at Davos.

00:32:37.660 --> 00:32:40.700
And a lot of the visitors,
participants sometimes

00:32:40.700 --> 00:32:43.270
were very intrigued
and try to guess where

00:32:43.270 --> 00:32:45.640
they could fly in the world.

00:32:45.640 --> 00:32:46.480
Thank you very much.

00:32:46.480 --> 00:32:49.952
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:17.232
Thank you.

00:33:28.720 --> 00:33:30.970
So I wanted to start out by
saying thank you to Lodo.

00:33:30.970 --> 00:33:32.950
It was a real pleasure
getting the invitation

00:33:32.950 --> 00:33:35.860
to come and speak
to you all tonight.

00:33:35.860 --> 00:33:38.920
Growing up, one of
my heroes in science

00:33:38.920 --> 00:33:41.770
was Richard Feynman, who
is a fantastic physicist

00:33:41.770 --> 00:33:44.860
but was always very
passionate about talking

00:33:44.860 --> 00:33:47.830
about how he spent time with
artists, because there were

00:33:47.830 --> 00:33:49.510
these two different
parts of your brain

00:33:49.510 --> 00:33:51.560
that needed to be exercised.

00:33:51.560 --> 00:33:54.070
And so he would sort of talk
physics with these folks

00:33:54.070 --> 00:33:56.230
and listen to their
work and art and felt

00:33:56.230 --> 00:33:58.360
that it made him a better
scientist as a result.

00:33:58.360 --> 00:34:00.820
And I've really felt
that way about talking

00:34:00.820 --> 00:34:02.860
about this project and
dealing with this project

00:34:02.860 --> 00:34:06.410
and was very, very
interested to participate.

00:34:06.410 --> 00:34:09.520
So one of the things that we
were asked to do this evening

00:34:09.520 --> 00:34:11.800
was tell you a little
bit about our research

00:34:11.800 --> 00:34:15.429
and how it was related
to the Aerocene project.

00:34:15.429 --> 00:34:17.560
And so I work in the
area of climate change,

00:34:17.560 --> 00:34:19.540
as John mentioned to start out.

00:34:19.540 --> 00:34:24.250
And so I'm not only a scientist,
but I'm also an avid historian.

00:34:24.250 --> 00:34:27.880
And so I love the history
behind the science that we do.

00:34:27.880 --> 00:34:30.380
And so I'm starting out here
with one of my favorite quotes,

00:34:30.380 --> 00:34:32.320
which is by George Santayana.

00:34:32.320 --> 00:34:34.780
"Those who cannot remember the
past are condemned to repeat

00:34:34.780 --> 00:34:35.280
it."

00:34:35.280 --> 00:34:38.270
And I think that this is
especially true of climate.

00:34:38.270 --> 00:34:40.360
And so what I
thought I'd start out

00:34:40.360 --> 00:34:43.270
by doing tonight is telling you
a little bit about the climate

00:34:43.270 --> 00:34:46.210
of our planet, the work that
we're doing in it, and then

00:34:46.210 --> 00:34:48.340
get back to how
I think we can do

00:34:48.340 --> 00:34:51.340
it better with the type of
technology which also happens

00:34:51.340 --> 00:34:54.350
to be the art that you're
hearing about this weekend.

00:34:54.350 --> 00:34:55.870
So this is the
temperature history

00:34:55.870 --> 00:34:57.010
of the Earth in the past.

00:34:57.010 --> 00:35:00.542
And this goes back almost
a million years in time.

00:35:00.542 --> 00:35:02.500
And one of the things
that you can see about it

00:35:02.500 --> 00:35:05.350
is that the Earth has been
warmer for periods of time,

00:35:05.350 --> 00:35:08.380
and it's been colder
for periods of time.

00:35:08.380 --> 00:35:12.220
And over the course of this
time, you can see that in blue

00:35:12.220 --> 00:35:15.700
here, these are ice ages when
the Earth has been much colder.

00:35:15.700 --> 00:35:18.040
There's also these
peaks in temperature

00:35:18.040 --> 00:35:19.810
when the Earth has been warmer.

00:35:19.810 --> 00:35:21.460
We should actually
be rather thankful

00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:22.990
that currently
we're living in what

00:35:22.990 --> 00:35:24.872
we call an interstitial period.

00:35:24.872 --> 00:35:26.830
And the average temperature
of our planet right

00:35:26.830 --> 00:35:29.710
now is about 57
degrees, which if we

00:35:29.710 --> 00:35:32.200
had the average temperature
in Boston the last few weeks,

00:35:32.200 --> 00:35:34.310
I think we'd all be quite happy.

00:35:34.310 --> 00:35:36.280
But over the course of
the year, over the whole

00:35:36.280 --> 00:35:38.740
of the planet that you just
saw in those beautiful images,

00:35:38.740 --> 00:35:42.340
it works out that we're
at about 57 degrees.

00:35:42.340 --> 00:35:44.140
And so one of the
things that you

00:35:44.140 --> 00:35:46.030
can ask about,
because we're talking

00:35:46.030 --> 00:35:49.000
about this idea of an
Anthropocene, the time period

00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:51.250
that we as humans
have affected--

00:35:51.250 --> 00:35:54.550
but the climate of our planet
really changes quite a bit

00:35:54.550 --> 00:35:55.630
over time.

00:35:55.630 --> 00:35:59.080
And you can wonder, how
much of this is natural?

00:35:59.080 --> 00:36:02.740
And it turns out that over
history, over almost 100 years

00:36:02.740 --> 00:36:05.200
ago now, people had worked
out that the temperature

00:36:05.200 --> 00:36:07.060
of our planet is very
variable, that you

00:36:07.060 --> 00:36:08.500
have to go through
these ice ages

00:36:08.500 --> 00:36:09.850
and these interstitial periods.

00:36:09.850 --> 00:36:12.490
And it has nothing to do
with us as humans being

00:36:12.490 --> 00:36:13.825
on the surface of the planet.

00:36:13.825 --> 00:36:16.450
Of course, we weren't there for
most of the time in that figure

00:36:16.450 --> 00:36:17.800
that I just showed you.

00:36:17.800 --> 00:36:20.830
And a very, very smart scientist
by the name of Milankovitch

00:36:20.830 --> 00:36:22.810
figured this out and
realized that this

00:36:22.810 --> 00:36:26.320
had to do with the orbital
mechanics of our planet, how

00:36:26.320 --> 00:36:28.540
our planet is
tilting on its axis

00:36:28.540 --> 00:36:33.470
when it's slightly further or
slightly closer to the sun.

00:36:33.470 --> 00:36:36.490
But if you zoom in on
that temperature record,

00:36:36.490 --> 00:36:39.700
you start to realize that
the temperature of our planet

00:36:39.700 --> 00:36:44.230
has changed a great deal over
just the last few generations.

00:36:44.230 --> 00:36:46.720
And this is highlighted
in blue on the figure

00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:48.200
that you can see here.

00:36:48.200 --> 00:36:50.230
So instead of these big
swings in temperature

00:36:50.230 --> 00:36:52.610
that took tens of
thousands of years,

00:36:52.610 --> 00:36:54.520
over just the last
100 years or so,

00:36:54.520 --> 00:36:57.250
the planet has gotten
a great deal warmer.

00:36:57.250 --> 00:37:00.490
The blue, actually, on here
is the instrumental record.

00:37:00.490 --> 00:37:01.930
It's the period
of time for which

00:37:01.930 --> 00:37:04.990
we've had accurate thermometers
to make measurements

00:37:04.990 --> 00:37:06.400
of climate.

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:07.870
And that should
give us some sense

00:37:07.870 --> 00:37:11.470
of how recently we've
only begun to understand

00:37:11.470 --> 00:37:12.880
the planet that we live on.

00:37:12.880 --> 00:37:14.970
It's only just over
about 100 years

00:37:14.970 --> 00:37:17.470
that we've been able to tell
about the climate of our planet

00:37:17.470 --> 00:37:20.050
and really understand it.

00:37:20.050 --> 00:37:22.750
By the way, that longest
record of temperature

00:37:22.750 --> 00:37:24.220
that we have for
the United States

00:37:24.220 --> 00:37:25.720
was actually made
just south of us.

00:37:25.720 --> 00:37:31.460
It was made in the Blue Hills
starting in about the 1880s.

00:37:31.460 --> 00:37:34.580
What you'll realize as opposed
to looking at the last plot

00:37:34.580 --> 00:37:37.610
that I showed you, where
temperatures swung wildly

00:37:37.610 --> 00:37:39.810
over these tens of
thousands of years,

00:37:39.810 --> 00:37:41.840
this is about the
length of human history.

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:43.400
Human history goes
back, of course,

00:37:43.400 --> 00:37:46.430
a few thousand years further
than this recorded history.

00:37:46.430 --> 00:37:49.375
But this is going back into
the ages of the Roman Empire.

00:37:49.375 --> 00:37:51.500
And what you'll notice
instead of these wild swings

00:37:51.500 --> 00:37:54.920
of many degrees of
temperature, instead,

00:37:54.920 --> 00:37:58.010
everything on this plot is
within about a half a degree.

00:37:58.010 --> 00:38:01.130
So that 57 degrees that I
said for the global average

00:38:01.130 --> 00:38:03.050
hasn't changed more than
about a half a degree

00:38:03.050 --> 00:38:04.740
in that period of time.

00:38:04.740 --> 00:38:06.530
And so one of the
points that we have

00:38:06.530 --> 00:38:08.120
to understand when
we start talking

00:38:08.120 --> 00:38:09.920
about the Anthropocene,
the effect

00:38:09.920 --> 00:38:12.020
that we've had on
the planet around us,

00:38:12.020 --> 00:38:16.160
is that we've grown as
a civilization in a very

00:38:16.160 --> 00:38:19.160
stable climate, perhaps
the most stable climate

00:38:19.160 --> 00:38:22.460
that this planet has seen
in over a million years.

00:38:22.460 --> 00:38:25.220
And so this is no coincidence
that this has happened.

00:38:25.220 --> 00:38:27.740
We've really benefited
from this and this ability

00:38:27.740 --> 00:38:30.410
to evolve in a world
that's very stable.

00:38:30.410 --> 00:38:34.290
But we're starting to
move outside of that.

00:38:34.290 --> 00:38:36.620
And so I mentioned that
I was a history buff.

00:38:36.620 --> 00:38:40.260
And so one of my
personal interests

00:38:40.260 --> 00:38:42.210
is understanding
how it is that we've

00:38:42.210 --> 00:38:44.850
been able to understand
how we're affecting

00:38:44.850 --> 00:38:46.480
the climate of our planet.

00:38:46.480 --> 00:38:48.960
And so this is one of
my favorite scientists.

00:38:48.960 --> 00:38:51.570
This is John Tyndall,
who is a Brit.

00:38:51.570 --> 00:38:55.080
And he was very interested in
building different instruments

00:38:55.080 --> 00:38:56.700
just like we do in
our lab, the one

00:38:56.700 --> 00:38:58.860
that John mentioned
a few moments ago,

00:38:58.860 --> 00:39:01.500
to understand how the
temperature of the planet

00:39:01.500 --> 00:39:03.175
is what it is.

00:39:03.175 --> 00:39:04.800
And so this is
something that he called

00:39:04.800 --> 00:39:06.540
a comparative spectrometer.

00:39:06.540 --> 00:39:09.780
And he was able to fill this
tube with different gases

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:13.470
and try to understand how they
interacted with solar radiation

00:39:13.470 --> 00:39:16.645
and infrared radiation, those
two things that are powering

00:39:16.645 --> 00:39:19.020
the Aerocene balloons that
you're going to be seeing more

00:39:19.020 --> 00:39:21.850
about in the next few days.

00:39:21.850 --> 00:39:25.140
And so what Tyndall realized
was that most of our atmosphere,

00:39:25.140 --> 00:39:27.450
most of what we breathe,
is nitrogen and oxygen.

00:39:27.450 --> 00:39:30.600
And it has no effect on the
temperature of our planet.

00:39:30.600 --> 00:39:33.000
But there's a few key
elements out there,

00:39:33.000 --> 00:39:35.220
carbon dioxide being
one of them, which

00:39:35.220 --> 00:39:37.170
is a so-called greenhouse gas.

00:39:37.170 --> 00:39:39.420
It absorbs that infrared
energy, the same thing

00:39:39.420 --> 00:39:42.060
that powers these
balloons, this MRI that you

00:39:42.060 --> 00:39:43.980
were hearing about at night.

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:46.470
And it allows that
energy to be trapped,

00:39:46.470 --> 00:39:48.300
much like a blanket
around the planet.

00:39:48.300 --> 00:39:52.693
And it raises the temperature
of the planet as a result.

00:39:52.693 --> 00:39:55.110
Now, mind you, from the dates
that you're seeing on there,

00:39:55.110 --> 00:39:58.410
Tyndall was doing his work
in the mid to late 1800s.

00:39:58.410 --> 00:40:00.030
And he had already
really figured out

00:40:00.030 --> 00:40:03.430
the basics of the climate
of our planet at that time.

00:40:03.430 --> 00:40:05.940
So if you ever hear that
climate change is something

00:40:05.940 --> 00:40:07.830
that we're just
figuring out right now,

00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:09.490
I'll talk about that
in a few moments.

00:40:09.490 --> 00:40:11.190
But really, the basics
of this were all

00:40:11.190 --> 00:40:14.280
worked out in the mid 1800s.

00:40:14.280 --> 00:40:16.440
And Tyndall's famous for
writing a paper about how

00:40:16.440 --> 00:40:18.273
if we didn't have these
wonderful greenhouse

00:40:18.273 --> 00:40:22.260
gases around our planet, we'd
be caught in an icy grip.

00:40:22.260 --> 00:40:24.540
And so that takes
us to the next part

00:40:24.540 --> 00:40:26.640
of our story, who
is a scientist that

00:40:26.640 --> 00:40:28.380
followed on Tyndall's work.

00:40:28.380 --> 00:40:29.940
This was Svante
Arrhenius, and he

00:40:29.940 --> 00:40:32.070
was working at the
end of the 1800s

00:40:32.070 --> 00:40:34.650
and the beginning of the 1900s.

00:40:34.650 --> 00:40:36.900
And he was really
intrigued by this idea

00:40:36.900 --> 00:40:39.150
that there were these materials
in our atmosphere that

00:40:39.150 --> 00:40:41.400
could make it warmer or colder.

00:40:41.400 --> 00:40:44.160
And so what Arrhenius
did was figure

00:40:44.160 --> 00:40:46.110
out how much warmer
the planet would

00:40:46.110 --> 00:40:48.930
be as a result of
having different amounts

00:40:48.930 --> 00:40:50.760
of these greenhouse gases.

00:40:50.760 --> 00:40:53.460
And so he looked at
doubling the amount of CO2,

00:40:53.460 --> 00:40:57.310
for example, and seeing how much
warmer the planet would get.

00:40:57.310 --> 00:40:58.900
And he wasn't
interested in doing

00:40:58.900 --> 00:41:00.442
this for altruistic purposes.

00:41:00.442 --> 00:41:01.900
You might think
that he was worried

00:41:01.900 --> 00:41:04.450
about greenhouse gases and
global warming, the things

00:41:04.450 --> 00:41:07.030
that we're talking about,
for example, at the COP21

00:41:07.030 --> 00:41:08.440
conference.

00:41:08.440 --> 00:41:10.690
Arrhenius was actually
from Scandinavia,

00:41:10.690 --> 00:41:13.240
and he was interested in trying
to make the planet warmer.

00:41:13.240 --> 00:41:15.730
The winters were too long
for him, much like us

00:41:15.730 --> 00:41:17.215
in Boston this year.

00:41:17.215 --> 00:41:19.090
And so he was very
interested in doing things

00:41:19.090 --> 00:41:21.910
like lighting coal on fire
to try to make the planet

00:41:21.910 --> 00:41:25.660
warmer as a result. So not
quite thinking about things

00:41:25.660 --> 00:41:28.355
the same way we do now.

00:41:28.355 --> 00:41:29.230
So what have we done?

00:41:29.230 --> 00:41:31.940
What is this Anthropocene
that we've been talking about?

00:41:31.940 --> 00:41:35.800
Well, largely, it's this input,
this burning of fossil fuels

00:41:35.800 --> 00:41:38.770
and the input of carbon dioxide
and other greenhouse gases

00:41:38.770 --> 00:41:40.330
to our atmosphere.

00:41:40.330 --> 00:41:42.130
And one of the results
that this has had

00:41:42.130 --> 00:41:44.150
is to warm the
temperature of the planet.

00:41:44.150 --> 00:41:46.240
So we're now warmer
than we probably

00:41:46.240 --> 00:41:49.090
have been in the last
several thousand years

00:41:49.090 --> 00:41:52.750
at least, perhaps many
tens of thousands of years.

00:41:52.750 --> 00:41:55.660
And so over that instrumental
record that I just told you

00:41:55.660 --> 00:41:58.730
about, the warmest years
on record from 1 to 20

00:41:58.730 --> 00:42:00.760
are on the upper right here.

00:42:00.760 --> 00:42:02.350
I haven't updated this recently.

00:42:02.350 --> 00:42:06.700
2017 just came in about a month
ago at number two on this plot.

00:42:06.700 --> 00:42:10.000
And so I often think about this
because when we tell people

00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:12.010
that the climate that
children will grow up in

00:42:12.010 --> 00:42:14.140
is different than the
climate that we grew up in,

00:42:14.140 --> 00:42:16.360
the climate that
we're experiencing now

00:42:16.360 --> 00:42:18.970
is already different than it
was when we were growing up.

00:42:18.970 --> 00:42:20.680
None of the first
years of my life

00:42:20.680 --> 00:42:23.890
are actually on the top
20 years on this plot.

00:42:23.890 --> 00:42:25.390
They all fall below that.

00:42:25.390 --> 00:42:26.950
The climate of the
last few decades

00:42:26.950 --> 00:42:28.300
has been radically different.

00:42:28.300 --> 00:42:31.270
This Anthropocene has led to
a radically different world

00:42:31.270 --> 00:42:33.970
already now that we're
living in than we experienced

00:42:33.970 --> 00:42:37.370
even a generation ago.

00:42:37.370 --> 00:42:40.210
So one of the things that
we do a great deal of

00:42:40.210 --> 00:42:43.520
is trying to understand exactly
what the climate of our planet

00:42:43.520 --> 00:42:44.020
is.

00:42:44.020 --> 00:42:46.750
And unfortunately, even
thanks to the work of Tyndall

00:42:46.750 --> 00:42:50.680
and Arrhenius back at the
late 1800s, early 1900s,

00:42:50.680 --> 00:42:52.780
they missed a few things
in their calculations.

00:42:52.780 --> 00:42:54.880
They weren't able to get
the climate of the planet

00:42:54.880 --> 00:42:56.050
quite right.

00:42:56.050 --> 00:42:57.670
And one of the reasons is that--

00:42:57.670 --> 00:43:00.640
this is actually a beautiful
image of the planet, something

00:43:00.640 --> 00:43:04.030
that I had on my wall as a child
from the Japanese Space Agency.

00:43:04.030 --> 00:43:07.080
But I think we already know, and
Tomás showed an earlier picture

00:43:07.080 --> 00:43:07.580
of this.

00:43:07.580 --> 00:43:09.524
What is this missing?

00:43:09.524 --> 00:43:10.520
Clouds.

00:43:10.520 --> 00:43:11.540
Clouds.

00:43:11.540 --> 00:43:13.340
The planet at any one
time should actually

00:43:13.340 --> 00:43:15.830
look like this, not
the other picture.

00:43:15.830 --> 00:43:18.440
And until we understand
the particulate matter

00:43:18.440 --> 00:43:21.710
in our atmosphere, we can't
understand the climate.

00:43:21.710 --> 00:43:26.960
So particles scatter some of
that incoming solar radiation,

00:43:26.960 --> 00:43:30.200
just like the greenhouse
gases are trapping

00:43:30.200 --> 00:43:32.750
some of those outgoing
terrestrial radiation,

00:43:32.750 --> 00:43:36.320
the infrared radiation that
was spoken about before.

00:43:36.320 --> 00:43:39.470
Beyond that, we've even changed
the properties of the clouds

00:43:39.470 --> 00:43:40.280
in our atmosphere.

00:43:40.280 --> 00:43:43.310
There's more clouds now
than there were earlier

00:43:43.310 --> 00:43:47.030
before this Anthropocene onset.

00:43:47.030 --> 00:43:48.800
And so understanding
all of these things

00:43:48.800 --> 00:43:51.140
is something that we
critically need to do.

00:43:51.140 --> 00:43:53.060
So with my last few
seconds, what I want to do

00:43:53.060 --> 00:43:55.518
is try to bring this back full
circle to one of the reasons

00:43:55.518 --> 00:43:57.620
that I'm so excited
about this project, which

00:43:57.620 --> 00:43:59.930
is making these measurements
in our atmosphere

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:01.730
is extremely costly.

00:44:01.730 --> 00:44:05.000
You can think about having to
go out and do research or get

00:44:05.000 --> 00:44:07.760
a research aircraft and fly
it at the cost of many tens

00:44:07.760 --> 00:44:09.320
of thousands of dollars.

00:44:09.320 --> 00:44:13.080
The idea of having these
incredible small platforms,

00:44:13.080 --> 00:44:15.590
things that can be sort of
crowdsourced to do measurements

00:44:15.590 --> 00:44:19.130
on a very local scale
but maybe in many places

00:44:19.130 --> 00:44:21.680
at the same time, can
really add to the knowledge

00:44:21.680 --> 00:44:23.990
that we currently
have about our planet,

00:44:23.990 --> 00:44:26.780
really help us reduce
that uncertainty

00:44:26.780 --> 00:44:29.940
in how climate is going to
be changing into the future.

00:44:29.940 --> 00:44:31.340
So with that, I'll
say thank you.

00:44:31.340 --> 00:44:32.840
I really appreciate
the chance to come out.

00:44:32.840 --> 00:44:34.840
And I'm looking forward
to the panel after this.

00:44:34.840 --> 00:44:38.144
[APPLAUSE]

00:44:53.388 --> 00:44:53.888
Thanks.

00:44:56.840 --> 00:44:59.690
Well, thank you to
Lodo and the rest

00:44:59.690 --> 00:45:02.780
of the organizers for the
opportunity to talk here.

00:45:02.780 --> 00:45:05.690
I first met Tomás, it must be
over five years ago when he was

00:45:05.690 --> 00:45:07.940
artist in residence here.

00:45:07.940 --> 00:45:11.270
And I was fascinated
immediately by the imagination

00:45:11.270 --> 00:45:15.200
and creativity of his sculptures
and also by his dedication

00:45:15.200 --> 00:45:20.450
to the problems faced by the
culture and by our environment.

00:45:20.450 --> 00:45:23.120
I did struggle to figure out
exactly what I was invited

00:45:23.120 --> 00:45:26.820
to talk about since I don't
work on either the Aerocene

00:45:26.820 --> 00:45:28.320
or directly on the atmosphere.

00:45:28.320 --> 00:45:31.250
But I have spent
the past 10 years

00:45:31.250 --> 00:45:33.590
studying, teaching,
and now writing

00:45:33.590 --> 00:45:39.110
about the uses, sources, and
systems that involve energy.

00:45:39.110 --> 00:45:42.110
So I thought I would
take some of the lessons

00:45:42.110 --> 00:45:44.780
from what I've
learned and taught

00:45:44.780 --> 00:45:47.630
and just provide
some basic picture

00:45:47.630 --> 00:45:49.520
about a couple of
issues that follow

00:45:49.520 --> 00:45:52.820
from the physics of energy.

00:45:52.820 --> 00:45:54.540
The two topics I'm
going to talk about,

00:45:54.540 --> 00:45:58.490
and I'm going to be very brief
and very not technical, I hope,

00:45:58.490 --> 00:46:01.280
are the scale of
the problem we face

00:46:01.280 --> 00:46:06.320
in trying to convert our culture
to sustainable energy sources

00:46:06.320 --> 00:46:08.570
and, secondly, a
couple of lessons

00:46:08.570 --> 00:46:11.600
from the second law
of thermodynamics.

00:46:11.600 --> 00:46:15.160
So first, let me
talk about scale.

00:46:15.160 --> 00:46:19.600
The tagline to talk about the
scale of the problem we face

00:46:19.600 --> 00:46:23.760
is the number 20 terawatts.

00:46:23.760 --> 00:46:25.050
This is the number--

00:46:25.050 --> 00:46:28.950
and it's useful to
spell it out in digits.

00:46:28.950 --> 00:46:34.050
This is the number that measures
the rate at which humanity uses

00:46:34.050 --> 00:46:39.300
primary energy, 20 times 10
to the 12th joules per second

00:46:39.300 --> 00:46:42.520
of primary energy.

00:46:42.520 --> 00:46:45.190
90% of this energy
comes from burning

00:46:45.190 --> 00:46:48.160
carbon and its
associated compounds,

00:46:48.160 --> 00:46:52.240
about a quarter of
it from petroleum,

00:46:52.240 --> 00:46:54.910
about a quarter of
it from coal, about

00:46:54.910 --> 00:46:57.280
a quarter of it
from natural gas,

00:46:57.280 --> 00:47:03.640
10% from biomass and
waste, and only about 6%

00:47:03.640 --> 00:47:08.500
from renewable hydropower.

00:47:08.500 --> 00:47:10.840
And hydropower has already
been developed to the extent

00:47:10.840 --> 00:47:11.770
it can be developed.

00:47:11.770 --> 00:47:15.730
That leaves about 2% left
for the modern renewables

00:47:15.730 --> 00:47:19.360
of wind and solar energy.

00:47:19.360 --> 00:47:22.800
I think 20 terawatts is a
hard number to comprehend.

00:47:22.800 --> 00:47:25.510
So I want to try to translate
it into some pictures

00:47:25.510 --> 00:47:31.270
and some images that will make
it more persuasive to you.

00:47:31.270 --> 00:47:33.880
This is a coal train
winding its way

00:47:33.880 --> 00:47:37.300
through the mountains
of the American west.

00:47:37.300 --> 00:47:41.980
And 20 terawatts is the energy
equivalent to 2 million tons

00:47:41.980 --> 00:47:45.820
of coal per hour fueling
our modern economy.

00:47:45.820 --> 00:47:48.760
I could have shown some pictures
of the skyline of Singapore

00:47:48.760 --> 00:47:51.250
or the docks of
Long Beach to try

00:47:51.250 --> 00:47:54.640
to show you the scope of the
enterprise that this fuels.

00:47:54.640 --> 00:47:57.400
But you have to appreciate
that this is an amazing scope.

00:47:57.400 --> 00:48:00.370
Now, we don't burn 20
million tons of coal.

00:48:00.370 --> 00:48:03.130
I've reduced the oil
and the natural gas

00:48:03.130 --> 00:48:06.040
and the biomass to the
equivalent amount of coal

00:48:06.040 --> 00:48:10.010
to just give you a picture of
the effort that's involved.

00:48:10.010 --> 00:48:14.210
To make this more concrete,
a typical coal train

00:48:14.210 --> 00:48:16.340
has about 120 cars.

00:48:16.340 --> 00:48:19.790
It's got about 120
tons of coal per car.

00:48:19.790 --> 00:48:22.880
That's 15,000 tons per train.

00:48:22.880 --> 00:48:27.170
And we need about 130 fully
loaded trains of that nature

00:48:27.170 --> 00:48:30.950
to supply the energy that our
civilization thrives on and is

00:48:30.950 --> 00:48:35.570
using in greater
amount all the time.

00:48:35.570 --> 00:48:37.260
I'm not going to let
go of this easily.

00:48:37.260 --> 00:48:42.560
Let me try to say
it another way.

00:48:42.560 --> 00:48:47.870
This is a large coal-fired
power plant in England.

00:48:47.870 --> 00:48:53.120
It uses about 3 gigawatts
worth of power constantly

00:48:53.120 --> 00:48:55.550
to produce about 1
gigawatt of electricity.

00:48:58.370 --> 00:49:02.240
In order to find an equivalent
to the carbon dioxide

00:49:02.240 --> 00:49:04.160
that we pump into
the atmosphere,

00:49:04.160 --> 00:49:08.270
it would be equivalent to 3,200
of these large coal-fired power

00:49:08.270 --> 00:49:15.182
plants operating continuously,
24 hours a day, all year long.

00:49:15.182 --> 00:49:17.390
That's the rate at which
we're putting carbon dioxide

00:49:17.390 --> 00:49:20.240
into the atmosphere.

00:49:20.240 --> 00:49:21.890
I'd rather think
about renewables.

00:49:21.890 --> 00:49:24.380
So let me try to convert this
number into something that

00:49:24.380 --> 00:49:26.810
relates to renewable energy.

00:49:26.810 --> 00:49:28.070
These are solar panels.

00:49:28.070 --> 00:49:30.500
They're the highest
efficiency way

00:49:30.500 --> 00:49:33.440
we have of gathering
solar energy in a very

00:49:33.440 --> 00:49:35.790
high-quality form.

00:49:35.790 --> 00:49:40.410
And to supply our
civilization with the energy

00:49:40.410 --> 00:49:42.030
that we require,
it would require

00:49:42.030 --> 00:49:45.660
a million square kilometers
of silicon photovoltaics

00:49:45.660 --> 00:49:48.870
at their current
efficiency spread out

00:49:48.870 --> 00:49:50.730
over the tropical deserts.

00:49:50.730 --> 00:49:53.190
And the tropical deserts are
chosen just because they're

00:49:53.190 --> 00:49:56.280
the sunniest
cloud-free areas where

00:49:56.280 --> 00:50:00.480
you could place solar panels
and get the most sunshine.

00:50:00.480 --> 00:50:02.520
The lesson I want
to take from this

00:50:02.520 --> 00:50:05.640
is that transforming society
to sustainable energy

00:50:05.640 --> 00:50:07.660
is heavy lifting.

00:50:07.660 --> 00:50:08.950
This is a huge problem.

00:50:08.950 --> 00:50:12.360
It's not going to be
solved unless we marshal

00:50:12.360 --> 00:50:16.000
the resources of our culture
and convince leaders,

00:50:16.000 --> 00:50:19.740
political leaders and
economists, that changes

00:50:19.740 --> 00:50:21.570
in behavior and
changes in investment

00:50:21.570 --> 00:50:25.110
have to be made over a very
short time span in order

00:50:25.110 --> 00:50:27.560
to bring a sustainable
energy revolution.

00:50:30.288 --> 00:50:31.830
The second thing I
want to talk about

00:50:31.830 --> 00:50:35.530
is the second law
of thermodynamics.

00:50:35.530 --> 00:50:38.710
And it's hard to talk to
non-technical audiences

00:50:38.710 --> 00:50:40.410
about the second law
of thermodynamics.

00:50:40.410 --> 00:50:43.770
So I want to try to say
it in non-technical form.

00:50:43.770 --> 00:50:48.240
The simplest non-technical form
is that there is no free lunch.

00:50:48.240 --> 00:50:51.000
Another way of saying it is
that disorder in the universe

00:50:51.000 --> 00:50:53.400
can only increase with time.

00:50:53.400 --> 00:50:54.870
But the implication
I want to talk

00:50:54.870 --> 00:50:59.670
about today is that
some forms of energy

00:50:59.670 --> 00:51:02.530
are more useful than others.

00:51:02.530 --> 00:51:04.590
The most useful
forms of energy are

00:51:04.590 --> 00:51:06.660
those that have what
we call zero entropy

00:51:06.660 --> 00:51:10.740
or very negligible entropy,
negligible disorder.

00:51:10.740 --> 00:51:14.910
And the two striking examples
are mechanical energy

00:51:14.910 --> 00:51:17.340
and electromagnetic energy.

00:51:17.340 --> 00:51:20.160
They can be translated
directly into useful work

00:51:20.160 --> 00:51:23.620
for humanity's needs.

00:51:23.620 --> 00:51:28.600
Thermal energy, heat,
is of varying quality

00:51:28.600 --> 00:51:31.700
depending upon its temperature.

00:51:31.700 --> 00:51:37.370
And sunlight is probably
the highest quality form

00:51:37.370 --> 00:51:39.540
of heat energy that's
available to us.

00:51:39.540 --> 00:51:43.460
It comes from the sun with a
temperature of 6,000 degrees

00:51:43.460 --> 00:51:46.490
Kelvin, which is about
10,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:51:46.490 --> 00:51:47.960
It's really hot.

00:51:47.960 --> 00:51:50.540
And that means it's
very high quality.

00:51:50.540 --> 00:51:52.880
In particular, the second
law of thermodynamics

00:51:52.880 --> 00:51:57.320
limits the efficiency at
which you can convert sunlight

00:51:57.320 --> 00:52:00.920
to the most usable form
of energy, electricity.

00:52:00.920 --> 00:52:04.460
And that limit is 95%.

00:52:04.460 --> 00:52:05.690
That's really wonderful.

00:52:05.690 --> 00:52:08.300
It means that sunlight
is a tremendous resource.

00:52:08.300 --> 00:52:12.260
It can be converted
directly into useful work

00:52:12.260 --> 00:52:15.900
at a 95% level.

00:52:15.900 --> 00:52:21.900
Well, we use solar panels
to collect light and turn it

00:52:21.900 --> 00:52:24.500
directly into
usable electricity.

00:52:24.500 --> 00:52:27.500
And there's been
research on solar panels

00:52:27.500 --> 00:52:28.730
for generations now.

00:52:28.730 --> 00:52:31.160
They're becoming cost
effective and competitive

00:52:31.160 --> 00:52:32.790
with fossil fuels.

00:52:32.790 --> 00:52:37.640
They're being mounted in large
solar farms across our country

00:52:37.640 --> 00:52:40.040
and elsewhere in the world.

00:52:40.040 --> 00:52:41.840
And their net
efficiency, the rate

00:52:41.840 --> 00:52:46.570
at which they turn solar energy
into electricity, is 6% to 7%.

00:52:50.100 --> 00:52:54.360
So we started with a
resource which is 95%

00:52:54.360 --> 00:52:55.838
potential useful energy.

00:52:55.838 --> 00:52:57.630
And what we can get
out of it with the best

00:52:57.630 --> 00:53:00.300
of our efficient
solar panels on a per

00:53:00.300 --> 00:53:04.420
square meter basis
is between 6% and 7%.

00:53:04.420 --> 00:53:07.990
So let me contrast
that with warmth.

00:53:07.990 --> 00:53:13.690
And the example I want to
use is ocean thermal energy.

00:53:13.690 --> 00:53:16.030
Now, people have known
for years that the surface

00:53:16.030 --> 00:53:19.270
of tropical oceans is warm
compared to the abyssal depths.

00:53:19.270 --> 00:53:21.460
In fact, the surface of
tropical oceans averages

00:53:21.460 --> 00:53:24.490
about 27 degrees
centigrade, and the depths

00:53:24.490 --> 00:53:28.360
are just a few degrees above
freezing, 5 degrees centigrade.

00:53:28.360 --> 00:53:31.630
There's a picture
here showing a slice

00:53:31.630 --> 00:53:35.590
through some tropical
ocean where you can see

00:53:35.590 --> 00:53:37.570
the very hot water on the top.

00:53:37.570 --> 00:53:41.350
And the depth with the
purple and magenta color

00:53:41.350 --> 00:53:43.940
indicates very cold water.

00:53:43.940 --> 00:53:45.380
And you can build an engine.

00:53:45.380 --> 00:53:47.840
Just like you can build an
engine that works on coal,

00:53:47.840 --> 00:53:49.700
you can build an engine
that takes that heat

00:53:49.700 --> 00:53:52.130
from the surface
waters, runs an engine,

00:53:52.130 --> 00:53:54.200
and dumps the extra
entropy that must

00:53:54.200 --> 00:53:57.770
be created, the extra
disorder, into the cold water

00:53:57.770 --> 00:54:00.450
at the depths.

00:54:00.450 --> 00:54:02.100
The same laws of
thermodynamics that

00:54:02.100 --> 00:54:04.020
allow solar energy
to be at most,

00:54:04.020 --> 00:54:09.150
theoretically, 95% efficient
allow ocean thermal energy

00:54:09.150 --> 00:54:10.900
to be 7% efficient.

00:54:10.900 --> 00:54:12.480
That's the best
you could do if you

00:54:12.480 --> 00:54:19.900
satisfied all the conditions of
efficiency that are available.

00:54:19.900 --> 00:54:21.970
So what's the situation?

00:54:21.970 --> 00:54:24.270
Well, there are devices
that have been proposed.

00:54:24.270 --> 00:54:26.700
And a couple have been
set up experimentally.

00:54:26.700 --> 00:54:30.000
This is a kind of cross
section to such a device that

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:33.585
takes warm water at the
surface, runs an engine,

00:54:33.585 --> 00:54:36.690
and dumps cold water at depth.

00:54:36.690 --> 00:54:38.610
And nobody's achieved
anything that

00:54:38.610 --> 00:54:41.220
can be measured as
an efficiency so far.

00:54:41.220 --> 00:54:44.250
So if 95% ramps down
to 7% by the time

00:54:44.250 --> 00:54:48.390
we're finished with our
crude ways of getting power,

00:54:48.390 --> 00:54:52.210
where's 7% going to take us?

00:54:52.210 --> 00:54:55.430
So I want to end on
an optimistic note.

00:54:55.430 --> 00:54:56.530
This is my last slide.

00:54:56.530 --> 00:55:00.610
I want to just emphasize
the promise of solar energy.

00:55:00.610 --> 00:55:02.800
This is a resource
that streams to Earth

00:55:02.800 --> 00:55:06.130
at about 20,000 times the
rate at which human beings use

00:55:06.130 --> 00:55:06.910
energy.

00:55:06.910 --> 00:55:08.500
It's high quality.

00:55:08.500 --> 00:55:10.210
It can be harvested efficiently.

00:55:10.210 --> 00:55:11.700
We don't know how
to do that yet.

00:55:11.700 --> 00:55:13.450
We don't know how to
store it effectively.

00:55:13.450 --> 00:55:15.520
And we don't know
how to transmit it

00:55:15.520 --> 00:55:17.500
as effectively as we should.

00:55:17.500 --> 00:55:20.110
But the resource is immense.

00:55:20.110 --> 00:55:22.900
And the conversion to electrical
energy at high efficiency

00:55:22.900 --> 00:55:24.130
is possible.

00:55:24.130 --> 00:55:27.160
And at moderate efficiency
is really within grasp.

00:55:27.160 --> 00:55:30.500
And it offers the promise for
a renewable energy future.

00:55:30.500 --> 00:55:31.339
Thank you.

00:55:31.339 --> 00:55:34.762
[APPLAUSE]

00:55:41.120 --> 00:55:47.370
So I'd like to invite the
panel up for discussion.

00:55:47.370 --> 00:55:50.160
So what we're going to do,
we have half an hour, right?

00:55:50.160 --> 00:55:50.690
OK.

00:55:50.690 --> 00:55:56.010
So what we're going to do is
we would love your questions,

00:55:56.010 --> 00:55:58.910
your comments, your thoughts.

00:55:58.910 --> 00:56:00.530
A room like this
is kind of funny

00:56:00.530 --> 00:56:02.480
because we're here in the front.

00:56:02.480 --> 00:56:03.355
You're back there.

00:56:03.355 --> 00:56:04.730
It really should
be in the round.

00:56:04.730 --> 00:56:06.750
We should be all sitting around
because we're in one room.

00:56:06.750 --> 00:56:08.690
We should all have a
discussion, actually.

00:56:08.690 --> 00:56:10.520
I'm going to start
it off, though,

00:56:10.520 --> 00:56:13.490
as you're thinking about your
questions and your comments.

00:56:13.490 --> 00:56:17.060
And when you do have a question,
please come up to the mics.

00:56:17.060 --> 00:56:20.280
Tell us your name and
ask your question.

00:56:20.280 --> 00:56:26.160
So I'm going to start off
with Tomás, appropriately.

00:56:26.160 --> 00:56:32.490
So Bob's thoughts
about a transition

00:56:32.490 --> 00:56:37.030
to a low-carbon world and
the numbers behind that

00:56:37.030 --> 00:56:38.080
are illuminating.

00:56:38.080 --> 00:56:44.660
And I always think of Bob as the
keeper of the laws of nature,

00:56:44.660 --> 00:56:45.920
right?

00:56:45.920 --> 00:56:46.560
I wish.

00:56:46.560 --> 00:56:51.440
And I'd actually really
like to hear more, Tomás,

00:56:51.440 --> 00:56:56.960
about the bridge to
a fossil-free future.

00:56:56.960 --> 00:56:58.220
You use that tagline.

00:56:58.220 --> 00:57:01.970
And actually, maybe Joaquin,
the work that you showed

00:57:01.970 --> 00:57:04.310
was really compelling
because there's

00:57:04.310 --> 00:57:09.260
a pathway that involves
citizen science and community

00:57:09.260 --> 00:57:12.110
engagement, which is
something completely different

00:57:12.110 --> 00:57:15.000
than necessarily the
technological and the science

00:57:15.000 --> 00:57:15.500
pathway.

00:57:15.500 --> 00:57:16.840
Could you talk a
little bit more?

00:57:16.840 --> 00:57:19.048
And Joaquin, if you want to
say a few words, as well.

00:57:21.370 --> 00:57:24.470
Yeah, I can-- it's working.

00:57:24.470 --> 00:57:28.300
No, I'm interested
in models of--

00:57:28.300 --> 00:57:32.750
Bob, you could maybe relay
this thermodynamic imagination

00:57:32.750 --> 00:57:38.860
that we are trying to pursue
in this elemental way of--

00:57:38.860 --> 00:57:42.040
because there is always this
coupling of metabolic regimes,

00:57:42.040 --> 00:57:45.400
how we have been living
on planet Earth, and how,

00:57:45.400 --> 00:57:51.110
in different times, humans
have evolved in relationship.

00:57:51.110 --> 00:57:53.212
How were they grasping
the resources, no?

00:57:53.212 --> 00:57:54.670
The first kind of
metabolic regime,

00:57:54.670 --> 00:57:56.740
we were hunter and
gatherers, nomads,

00:57:56.740 --> 00:57:58.090
tribes around the world.

00:57:58.090 --> 00:58:01.060
We would always follow the
season, follow the sun, right?

00:58:01.060 --> 00:58:03.160
Then we invented agriculture.

00:58:03.160 --> 00:58:04.600
We became sedentary.

00:58:04.600 --> 00:58:06.820
The second metabolic,
we domesticated the sun,

00:58:06.820 --> 00:58:08.170
domesticated the animals.

00:58:08.170 --> 00:58:11.350
And somehow, we managed
to become sedentary

00:58:11.350 --> 00:58:12.430
in a certain area.

00:58:12.430 --> 00:58:15.430
In the third
metabolic regime, we

00:58:15.430 --> 00:58:18.850
learned how to extract
more from the sun.

00:58:18.850 --> 00:58:20.920
And we learned how
to burn charcoal,

00:58:20.920 --> 00:58:22.300
which somehow a rock--

00:58:22.300 --> 00:58:24.400
how many millions
of years of sun

00:58:24.400 --> 00:58:27.280
influence it needed to
then be able to burn?

00:58:27.280 --> 00:58:30.100
This means we start to have
all these extractive economies,

00:58:30.100 --> 00:58:33.310
which somehow also
expanded civilization

00:58:33.310 --> 00:58:36.700
to become again this kind of--

00:58:36.700 --> 00:58:40.530
I think how much population
are living on the cities.

00:58:40.530 --> 00:58:43.270
The fourth metabolic regime
that somehow we are proposing

00:58:43.270 --> 00:58:46.360
is like what happened
when these extractive

00:58:46.360 --> 00:58:49.540
economies and all these
resources and also the way of--

00:58:49.540 --> 00:58:51.760
because every metabolic
regime somehow also

00:58:51.760 --> 00:58:55.840
has allowed different modes
of aggregation of people.

00:58:55.840 --> 00:58:58.210
When we were nomads, the
tribes cannot grow to a certain

00:58:58.210 --> 00:58:58.810
extent.

00:58:58.810 --> 00:59:00.220
Then the other ones keep
growing, keep growing.

00:59:00.220 --> 00:59:01.762
This means the
relationship we always

00:59:01.762 --> 00:59:03.760
have established of
how we have related

00:59:03.760 --> 00:59:06.780
our society with the sun
through these metabolic regimes,

00:59:06.780 --> 00:59:09.670
they always give products
of transportation,

00:59:09.670 --> 00:59:12.070
mobility, and so forth.

00:59:12.070 --> 00:59:14.620
These are rather
speculation things.

00:59:14.620 --> 00:59:19.030
Because what it does
is it crosses this idea

00:59:19.030 --> 00:59:20.530
of very elemental, no?

00:59:20.530 --> 00:59:21.730
The sun is heated up.

00:59:21.730 --> 00:59:23.770
You don't need a solar
panel, a battery,

00:59:23.770 --> 00:59:25.900
or convert it to
electricity directly.

00:59:25.900 --> 00:59:29.440
You get to maybe something
I like to speculate.

00:59:29.440 --> 00:59:33.130
After Homo sapiens, maybe
we became Homo floatantes.

00:59:33.130 --> 00:59:35.890
And we became numb at the
beginning of the atmosphere.

00:59:35.890 --> 00:59:39.460
And you might think, oh, we were
fishes floating in the water.

00:59:39.460 --> 00:59:41.090
Why we moved to the land?

00:59:41.090 --> 00:59:43.780
And what would be the
benefit to move into the air?

00:59:43.780 --> 00:59:45.160
This means we live there.

00:59:45.160 --> 00:59:50.660
But really try to
maybe get it around.

00:59:50.660 --> 00:59:51.970
Anyone else want to say?

00:59:51.970 --> 00:59:53.970
Well, maybe I just
would add one thing,

00:59:53.970 --> 00:59:56.880
that I think that a
transformation of society's

00:59:56.880 --> 00:59:59.280
way of thinking about
its culture and energy

00:59:59.280 --> 01:00:02.370
is a really important
part of dealing

01:00:02.370 --> 01:00:05.280
with the energy transfer,
the transformation

01:00:05.280 --> 01:00:06.810
to renewable energy.

01:00:06.810 --> 01:00:09.630
We demand that we can turn
on our washing machine

01:00:09.630 --> 01:00:11.340
at any time of day.

01:00:11.340 --> 01:00:15.270
We expect to be able to take
a car wherever we want to go.

01:00:15.270 --> 01:00:19.980
And the variability
of energy sources

01:00:19.980 --> 01:00:23.220
that you have to deal with,
for example, with solar energy

01:00:23.220 --> 01:00:27.000
versus infrared energy,
if human beings adapted

01:00:27.000 --> 01:00:29.760
to the cadences at which
energy is available,

01:00:29.760 --> 01:00:31.500
it would make a
big impact on what

01:00:31.500 --> 01:00:36.460
we have to do in order to
reach a sustainable future.

01:00:36.460 --> 01:00:38.260
So please come up to the mics.

01:00:38.260 --> 01:00:41.610
I actually want to
follow up with Joaquin.

01:00:41.610 --> 01:00:45.090
I'm going to ask you to
say some words because I'm

01:00:45.090 --> 01:00:49.860
very curious about
a particular thing.

01:00:49.860 --> 01:00:55.860
So the outcomes that
you are after when

01:00:55.860 --> 01:00:58.080
engaging the community,
especially a community that

01:00:58.080 --> 01:01:02.280
is under pressure from
environmental conditions,

01:01:02.280 --> 01:01:03.870
right?

01:01:03.870 --> 01:01:05.970
What happens?

01:01:05.970 --> 01:01:09.420
What are the actions
that the community takes?

01:01:09.420 --> 01:01:10.560
All right.

01:01:10.560 --> 01:01:14.100
To begin with, I'm
going to be honest.

01:01:14.100 --> 01:01:18.180
Sometimes I feel a little
pessimistic about weather

01:01:18.180 --> 01:01:20.590
and climate change.

01:01:20.590 --> 01:01:23.400
I have a few friends in
Harvard who are architects

01:01:23.400 --> 01:01:24.690
and are actually here.

01:01:24.690 --> 01:01:28.950
And yesterday, we were
talking about what we called

01:01:28.950 --> 01:01:32.040
the brace for impact scenario.

01:01:32.040 --> 01:01:36.390
And I was asking them,
when is that coming?

01:01:36.390 --> 01:01:38.760
And we realized
that we are already

01:01:38.760 --> 01:01:41.910
living the brace
for impact scenario

01:01:41.910 --> 01:01:47.370
because of the amount of
times we are seeing all

01:01:47.370 --> 01:01:51.010
these extreme weather events.

01:01:51.010 --> 01:01:55.950
And so in terms of
community, I think

01:01:55.950 --> 01:01:59.190
this is going to
change us a lot.

01:01:59.190 --> 01:02:02.970
We are going to have to
find new ways to relate

01:02:02.970 --> 01:02:08.550
to each other, new ways to help
each other, new ways to learn

01:02:08.550 --> 01:02:10.270
from each other.

01:02:10.270 --> 01:02:16.680
And this is why I
envisage this future

01:02:16.680 --> 01:02:22.420
of technological developments
that might seem basic

01:02:22.420 --> 01:02:24.660
but might actually
be very helpful,

01:02:24.660 --> 01:02:30.090
like this idea of creating
a solar aerial mesh network.

01:02:30.090 --> 01:02:32.760
And I imagine places
where there have

01:02:32.760 --> 01:02:34.770
been floods in the past years.

01:02:34.770 --> 01:02:40.770
And how do you deploy
a relief efforts team

01:02:40.770 --> 01:02:44.380
to a place that has
suffered a natural disaster?

01:02:44.380 --> 01:02:49.920
And this is where I think
of this network that

01:02:49.920 --> 01:02:54.550
runs on clean solar energy.

01:02:54.550 --> 01:03:00.030
But just to sum
it up, I very much

01:03:00.030 --> 01:03:05.670
agree with Bob of the
sheer size of the problem.

01:03:05.670 --> 01:03:10.870
And actually, each of
us has a role in this.

01:03:10.870 --> 01:03:16.590
And maybe we can start
thinking how to live

01:03:16.590 --> 01:03:19.530
more energy-efficient life.

01:03:19.530 --> 01:03:21.190
So thanks.

01:03:21.190 --> 01:03:21.690
Thank you.

01:03:21.690 --> 01:03:23.370
Questions?

01:03:23.370 --> 01:03:24.360
No one's stepping up.

01:03:24.360 --> 01:03:25.732
Yeah, Caroline?

01:03:30.460 --> 01:03:34.390
So this may be a challenge
for my dear friend Tomás.

01:03:34.390 --> 01:03:37.300
But I would be
interested in the others.

01:03:37.300 --> 01:03:41.730
The human architectural
fascination with life

01:03:41.730 --> 01:03:43.600
in a capsule.

01:03:43.600 --> 01:03:49.150
So the sky city, the cloud
city, the Bucky Fuller spaceship

01:03:49.150 --> 01:03:54.080
city, these are, in
fact, incredibly energy

01:03:54.080 --> 01:03:56.180
intensive, right?

01:03:56.180 --> 01:04:00.950
They are the sort of emotional
manifestation of freedom

01:04:00.950 --> 01:04:07.700
that is the result of
energy-intensive manufacturing

01:04:07.700 --> 01:04:15.110
processes, delivery systems,
infrastructures of power

01:04:15.110 --> 01:04:18.780
that are somehow brought up
there as batteries or whatever.

01:04:18.780 --> 01:04:25.230
So in a way, it's a challenge
to the nomadic fantasy

01:04:25.230 --> 01:04:31.950
and how this is coupled with
the very elaborated technologies

01:04:31.950 --> 01:04:36.610
that would make such
a cloud city possible.

01:04:36.610 --> 01:04:38.520
It's a tough question.

01:04:38.520 --> 01:04:40.930
It's like tough love.

01:04:40.930 --> 01:04:43.780
No, I think so.

01:04:43.780 --> 01:04:44.590
I think so.

01:04:44.590 --> 01:04:47.780
It's so simple as you can
start to reuse a plastic bag.

01:04:47.780 --> 01:04:50.200
Instead of downgrading
material, you

01:04:50.200 --> 01:04:53.380
upscale the relationship
you have with plastic bags.

01:04:53.380 --> 01:04:57.910
I'm very happy that now plastic
bags through [INAUDIBLE]

01:04:57.910 --> 01:05:01.360
into community suddenly
can give another lifespan,

01:05:01.360 --> 01:05:05.200
or the lifespan corresponds
with the ability of themself

01:05:05.200 --> 01:05:08.080
to be within the
environment we have created.

01:05:08.080 --> 01:05:11.080
Or the other metaphor
is the city in a ball.

01:05:11.080 --> 01:05:13.370
OK, now let's put it away.

01:05:13.370 --> 01:05:16.900
Oh, Juan Rodriguez is there.

01:05:16.900 --> 01:05:21.070
Now, first, I don't know.

01:05:21.070 --> 01:05:24.760
I would almost say it's like
the idea of, in [INAUDIBLE],,

01:05:24.760 --> 01:05:29.170
we are condemned to
live in a sublunar.

01:05:29.170 --> 01:05:31.810
The Bible is somehow
planet Earth today.

01:05:31.810 --> 01:05:33.850
And we have a kind of
an environmental justice

01:05:33.850 --> 01:05:36.640
responsibility, also, the
amount of people who are onboard

01:05:36.640 --> 01:05:38.170
on this Spaceship Earth.

01:05:38.170 --> 01:05:40.160
And Bruno doesn't
like an analogy.

01:05:40.160 --> 01:05:42.220
But somehow, I think, so
when you understand also

01:05:42.220 --> 01:05:43.870
the constraint of
where we are living,

01:05:43.870 --> 01:05:46.390
you are living in a bubble
to a certain extent, right?

01:05:46.390 --> 01:05:48.580
And there are some
resources that somehow maybe

01:05:48.580 --> 01:05:50.580
you can start to
relate differently.

01:05:50.580 --> 01:05:53.240
There is this kind
of logic on that.

01:05:53.240 --> 01:05:55.270
The other is also like
when you think in the way

01:05:55.270 --> 01:05:57.898
that I propose, I
think, it's like--

01:05:57.898 --> 01:05:59.440
and I think what
the other thing does

01:05:59.440 --> 01:06:01.780
is also how to fly with
the feet on the ground.

01:06:01.780 --> 01:06:05.260
This means there is a
huge responsibility.

01:06:05.260 --> 01:06:08.020
So when you scale it too
fast, too quickly, then you

01:06:08.020 --> 01:06:09.880
end up in a modernist dream.

01:06:09.880 --> 01:06:13.900
But the way of how we are
doing the things today here

01:06:13.900 --> 01:06:17.150
with Joaquin, with Lodo,
with the measurement,

01:06:17.150 --> 01:06:18.758
the quality of the
air, well, let's

01:06:18.758 --> 01:06:20.050
give it a little bit more time.

01:06:20.050 --> 01:06:22.450
Phil had also said there
is no lack of energy.

01:06:22.450 --> 01:06:24.250
There is no energy crisis.

01:06:24.250 --> 01:06:26.590
There is a crisis of
imagination and how

01:06:26.590 --> 01:06:29.890
we can couple and recouple
ourself with the environment.

01:06:29.890 --> 01:06:31.570
So the relationship
of trying to say

01:06:31.570 --> 01:06:35.680
is like, oh, how complex is--
when I was at NASA in 2009,

01:06:35.680 --> 01:06:37.870
people had put
people in the moon.

01:06:37.870 --> 01:06:40.210
And I said, for 300
euro, you collect

01:06:40.210 --> 01:06:42.610
a couple of plastic bags,
and you can lift up yourself

01:06:42.610 --> 01:06:43.277
from the ground.

01:06:43.277 --> 01:06:45.220
They cannot believe it.

01:06:45.220 --> 01:06:47.110
There is somehow
such an addiction

01:06:47.110 --> 01:06:49.390
of people of thinking
the infrastructure

01:06:49.390 --> 01:06:50.980
you need to do such a thing--

01:06:50.980 --> 01:06:53.770
that is unbelievable for
them to get out of this.

01:06:53.770 --> 01:06:55.840
And any time that
you demonstrate,

01:06:55.840 --> 01:06:58.657
these people say, oh,
there's some kind of magic.

01:06:58.657 --> 01:06:59.740
Now, I challenge yourself.

01:06:59.740 --> 01:07:01.780
And let's put it away.

01:07:01.780 --> 01:07:05.170
And you know that
this is quite cheap.

01:07:05.170 --> 01:07:06.730
We are really
working now seriously

01:07:06.730 --> 01:07:10.190
on something which is a
300-meter sphere, diameter,

01:07:10.190 --> 01:07:15.220
which can be able to lift up
a couple of hundred people up

01:07:15.220 --> 01:07:17.740
into the air, rigid sphere.

01:07:17.740 --> 01:07:20.890
And it's incredibly
cheap, if I tell you.

01:07:20.890 --> 01:07:22.400
And the infrastructure
is something

01:07:22.400 --> 01:07:25.570
that even an artist can
understand and reteach

01:07:25.570 --> 01:07:28.000
to a whole community of
scientists or re-engage

01:07:28.000 --> 01:07:32.500
onto understanding how we can
sense the sun differently.

01:07:32.500 --> 01:07:34.555
I mean, just only--

01:07:34.555 --> 01:07:35.680
I mean, with Lodo, we know.

01:07:35.680 --> 01:07:38.050
But I've been with
director of NASA and people

01:07:38.050 --> 01:07:40.720
at [INAUDIBLE] saying, what is
the average on a jet stream?

01:07:40.720 --> 01:07:42.790
They don't know it.

01:07:42.790 --> 01:07:45.310
How long it will take you
to make it around the world?

01:07:45.310 --> 01:07:48.460
Actually, only what you know is
like, OK, in the current plane,

01:07:48.460 --> 01:07:50.470
you got to your
destination half an hour

01:07:50.470 --> 01:07:51.790
early if the wind was blowing.

01:07:51.790 --> 01:07:53.830
But now if you are
drifting, if you

01:07:53.830 --> 01:07:57.640
are in a kind of
drift situation,

01:07:57.640 --> 01:07:59.260
it takes quite an effort.

01:07:59.260 --> 01:08:03.280
And I think so it's not
right to all the time

01:08:03.280 --> 01:08:06.760
condemn some idea for
thinking that infrastructure

01:08:06.760 --> 01:08:07.610
is so gigantic.

01:08:07.610 --> 01:08:09.140
Well, actually,
it's very simple.

01:08:09.140 --> 01:08:13.104
It's simple as a plastic bag
in the hands of somebody else.

01:08:13.104 --> 01:08:14.080
Great answer.

01:08:14.080 --> 01:08:16.240
Yep.

01:08:16.240 --> 01:08:16.740
Please.

01:08:24.410 --> 01:08:28.240
So my question is regarding
the solar balloons

01:08:28.240 --> 01:08:30.430
that you guys talked about.

01:08:30.430 --> 01:08:33.370
So assuming that you
will be able to launch

01:08:33.370 --> 01:08:36.790
these balloons at the
location that you like to,

01:08:36.790 --> 01:08:39.100
what is the vision?

01:08:39.100 --> 01:08:41.680
What is the plan if you
collect all this data

01:08:41.680 --> 01:08:43.510
all across the planet?

01:08:43.510 --> 01:08:46.390
Then where does that
data go, and what are you

01:08:46.390 --> 01:08:48.050
expecting to get out of it?

01:08:48.050 --> 01:08:50.710
What is the plan for?

01:08:50.710 --> 01:08:53.439
The panel could speak
to it a little bit.

01:08:53.439 --> 01:09:01.100
The second question is for the
efficiency of the solar power.

01:09:01.100 --> 01:09:04.810
So I want to find out
what is the bottleneck

01:09:04.810 --> 01:09:07.270
not to have higher efficiency.

01:09:07.270 --> 01:09:11.500
And so what is the
efficiency that we

01:09:11.500 --> 01:09:16.750
are aiming for to replace that
130 coal trains per hours?

01:09:16.750 --> 01:09:21.270
So can you give us a sense
what we are looking at there?

01:09:21.270 --> 01:09:24.540
I can start with the
collection of the data.

01:09:24.540 --> 01:09:27.263
As I mentioned before, there
are different types of data

01:09:27.263 --> 01:09:27.930
you can collect.

01:09:27.930 --> 01:09:30.600
If you go higher up in the
stratosphere, normally,

01:09:30.600 --> 01:09:33.390
you would be
monitoring the ozone.

01:09:33.390 --> 01:09:36.930
The ozone layer is very
important for life on Earth.

01:09:36.930 --> 01:09:39.770
And if you have a
depletion of ozone,

01:09:39.770 --> 01:09:46.350
you can get the [INAUDIBLE]
light which comes to Earth,

01:09:46.350 --> 01:09:49.569
and it would be very damaging.

01:09:49.569 --> 01:09:52.710
So this is why the monitoring
to make sure that we-- we

01:09:52.710 --> 01:09:55.620
had an example of an
ozone hole not long ago.

01:09:55.620 --> 01:09:57.970
We're just recovering
out of that.

01:09:57.970 --> 01:10:00.270
So it's the kind of
monitoring to make sure.

01:10:00.270 --> 01:10:04.260
But there are other gases, like
Dan has been mentioning about,

01:10:04.260 --> 01:10:08.940
like CO2, methane, which are
very important as a greenhouse.

01:10:08.940 --> 01:10:13.440
So the increase of those
gases makes the temperature

01:10:13.440 --> 01:10:15.790
on Earth increase enormously.

01:10:15.790 --> 01:10:17.820
And so we want to
monitor to make sure

01:10:17.820 --> 01:10:20.110
that those are not
increasing too much

01:10:20.110 --> 01:10:22.380
and what is the
trend that we notice.

01:10:22.380 --> 01:10:24.780
So those are the upper level.

01:10:24.780 --> 01:10:27.390
Low level with a
tethered balloon,

01:10:27.390 --> 01:10:29.560
you can actually
monitor the air quality.

01:10:29.560 --> 01:10:31.270
So all this particles
I was talking about

01:10:31.270 --> 01:10:34.720
and chemicals are very
damaging to your health.

01:10:34.720 --> 01:10:37.170
So they're the result
mainly of combustion.

01:10:37.170 --> 01:10:40.380
They can produce acid
rain And there are also

01:10:40.380 --> 01:10:42.270
particles at cell
phone dimensions

01:10:42.270 --> 01:10:46.390
that can be very, very
bad for your health.

01:10:46.390 --> 01:10:48.750
So the quality of
the air locally

01:10:48.750 --> 01:10:50.460
is very important
for where you live.

01:10:50.460 --> 01:10:54.510
And if it's monitored,
in the summertime,

01:10:54.510 --> 01:10:59.820
you have a lot of sometimes
pollutant trapped in the lower

01:10:59.820 --> 01:11:00.570
troposphere.

01:11:00.570 --> 01:11:01.500
And it can be very--

01:11:01.500 --> 01:11:04.410
I come from a region
of Milan where

01:11:04.410 --> 01:11:06.150
you have very poor air quality.

01:11:06.150 --> 01:11:08.970
We have heard about Beijing
and all these big cities.

01:11:08.970 --> 01:11:13.670
So they can be very
efficient in measuring

01:11:13.670 --> 01:11:17.940
what is the chemistry of those
lower tropospheric layers,

01:11:17.940 --> 01:11:22.270
what's going on there, and how
we can control the pollution.

01:11:22.270 --> 01:11:25.770
So there are different types
of monitoring we can do.

01:11:25.770 --> 01:11:28.470
To follow up on
what Lodo just said,

01:11:28.470 --> 01:11:31.920
another thing that we've really
struggled with as scientists

01:11:31.920 --> 01:11:35.040
over the last couple of
decades is shrinking budgets.

01:11:35.040 --> 01:11:37.680
And there's really only a
handful of research aircraft

01:11:37.680 --> 01:11:39.930
that we can do these
high-quality measurements with,

01:11:39.930 --> 01:11:43.200
especially talking about
things like the ozone layer.

01:11:43.200 --> 01:11:47.970
We're still relying on Cold
War-era aircraft, ER-2s,

01:11:47.970 --> 01:11:51.210
the old U-2 spy planes
that have been retasked,

01:11:51.210 --> 01:11:52.180
things like that.

01:11:52.180 --> 01:11:55.530
They cost many tens of
thousands of dollars per hour.

01:11:55.530 --> 01:11:58.770
They're absolutely necessary
to validate new satellites that

01:11:58.770 --> 01:12:01.980
go up to understand the quality
of the ozone to make sure

01:12:01.980 --> 01:12:04.380
that the ozone hole is, indeed,
still closing, something

01:12:04.380 --> 01:12:06.060
that we are just getting out of.

01:12:06.060 --> 01:12:08.790
And so the idea that we might
have these platforms that

01:12:08.790 --> 01:12:13.290
could allow us to do this at
much lower cost in many more

01:12:13.290 --> 01:12:17.730
locations is really
intriguing and the surface

01:12:17.730 --> 01:12:19.620
measurements, as well.

01:12:19.620 --> 01:12:22.920
We're notoriously bad about
making most of our measurements

01:12:22.920 --> 01:12:25.440
in the northern hemisphere,
where most of the developed

01:12:25.440 --> 01:12:28.210
countries are, and
notoriously bad about making

01:12:28.210 --> 01:12:30.210
too few measurements in
the southern hemisphere.

01:12:30.210 --> 01:12:32.940
And this idea of
low-cost platforms

01:12:32.940 --> 01:12:35.040
that we could look at
these types of problems,

01:12:35.040 --> 01:12:37.300
understand air
quality, for example,

01:12:37.300 --> 01:12:39.570
in some of the large cities
in the southern hemisphere

01:12:39.570 --> 01:12:42.620
is something that we critically
need to do as scientists.

01:12:42.620 --> 01:12:46.050
Could I add that, for
example, Joaquin's example,

01:12:46.050 --> 01:12:50.610
he's talking about communities
outside Buenos Aires, which

01:12:50.610 --> 01:12:53.670
are very badly
affected by pollution.

01:12:53.670 --> 01:12:55.140
You can see them.

01:12:55.140 --> 01:12:57.040
We had an example
like that in Venice.

01:12:57.040 --> 01:12:58.530
Venice is a beautiful city.

01:12:58.530 --> 01:13:01.050
When I was growing up,
you will approach Venice,

01:13:01.050 --> 01:13:05.910
and you will have a huge
chemical plant there.

01:13:05.910 --> 01:13:07.590
And there was terrible quality.

01:13:07.590 --> 01:13:09.900
So they probably are
in the same situation.

01:13:09.900 --> 01:13:12.670
But you can improve
that with rules.

01:13:12.670 --> 01:13:17.950
And your health is badly
affected by the pollution.

01:13:17.950 --> 01:13:21.180
So the idea is just to
prove that to the government

01:13:21.180 --> 01:13:22.890
so that they can do
something about it.

01:13:22.890 --> 01:13:26.460
No, you do it by yourself.

01:13:26.460 --> 01:13:28.320
There are people
collecting data.

01:13:28.320 --> 01:13:29.160
We collect it.

01:13:29.160 --> 01:13:31.620
We will do the
experiment on Saturday.

01:13:31.620 --> 01:13:33.040
We can collect data.

01:13:33.040 --> 01:13:34.500
You can have communities.

01:13:34.500 --> 01:13:36.240
Joaquin is trying
to make a community

01:13:36.240 --> 01:13:39.930
responsible for trying to
collect some more information.

01:13:39.930 --> 01:13:45.940
And then the data itself, if
you can show that it is so bad,

01:13:45.940 --> 01:13:48.070
you can force some
political action.

01:13:48.070 --> 01:13:48.570
Yeah.

01:13:48.570 --> 01:13:50.670
There is a group we work
which is called-- well,

01:13:50.670 --> 01:13:53.250
beside being [INAUDIBLE],,
part of Red Cross,

01:13:53.250 --> 01:13:55.110
also, which they
kind of engage also.

01:13:55.110 --> 01:13:56.610
Within the project,
there is a group

01:13:56.610 --> 01:13:58.470
which is called Public Lab.

01:13:58.470 --> 01:14:00.510
They do a lot of
environmental justice.

01:14:00.510 --> 01:14:02.050
And it's been in
the hands of them.

01:14:02.050 --> 01:14:04.320
They provide a lot of
downgrading or kind

01:14:04.320 --> 01:14:06.790
of low-scale monitoring
devices, which

01:14:06.790 --> 01:14:10.200
we hope so will work together
with high scientists,

01:14:10.200 --> 01:14:13.290
with low communities, also,
coming up together with.

01:14:13.290 --> 01:14:15.330
And they are building
huge cases, also,

01:14:15.330 --> 01:14:18.150
to big multinational,
also, in the case of Shell,

01:14:18.150 --> 01:14:20.950
which is coming up
in the [INAUDIBLE],,

01:14:20.950 --> 01:14:22.230
what Joaquin is talking.

01:14:22.230 --> 01:14:25.326
You can build up huge cases on
trying to measure and give it

01:14:25.326 --> 01:14:27.180
in the hands of them
the way you have

01:14:27.180 --> 01:14:30.390
to monitor the environment.

01:14:30.390 --> 01:14:32.640
I think I owe you an
answer to the second part

01:14:32.640 --> 01:14:33.570
of your question.

01:14:33.570 --> 01:14:34.180
Please.

01:14:34.180 --> 01:14:35.680
Really, there are
two things to say.

01:14:35.680 --> 01:14:41.790
The first is that 7% as a final
efficiency for solar energy

01:14:41.790 --> 01:14:43.800
is not as bad as it may sound.

01:14:43.800 --> 01:14:46.860
It's more that
it's a bad starting

01:14:46.860 --> 01:14:48.620
place for ocean thermal energy.

01:14:51.840 --> 01:14:53.430
I was writing an
article about this,

01:14:53.430 --> 01:14:56.190
and I said a million square
kilometers is half the surface

01:14:56.190 --> 01:14:57.870
area of Libya.

01:14:57.870 --> 01:14:59.520
And the person I
was writing it for

01:14:59.520 --> 01:15:01.260
said, people don't
know how big Libya is,

01:15:01.260 --> 01:15:06.930
so maybe you should convert
it to Texas plus Oklahoma.

01:15:06.930 --> 01:15:11.550
So then it becomes a problem
of storage and transmission.

01:15:11.550 --> 01:15:14.670
People are very refractory
about having transmission lines

01:15:14.670 --> 01:15:16.710
strung across their landscape.

01:15:16.710 --> 01:15:21.420
But high-voltage DC electric
transmission is very efficient

01:15:21.420 --> 01:15:24.240
and could carry electricity
from far-away places

01:15:24.240 --> 01:15:26.500
to where it's needed.

01:15:26.500 --> 01:15:32.400
The second is, how did
95% get down to 7%?

01:15:32.400 --> 01:15:34.290
Some people in the
audience may be

01:15:34.290 --> 01:15:38.670
perplexed because you can
read that the efficiency of PV

01:15:38.670 --> 01:15:42.190
modules now is about 20%.

01:15:42.190 --> 01:15:44.680
But when you lay them
out on the landscape,

01:15:44.680 --> 01:15:46.360
they have to be
tilted at an angle.

01:15:46.360 --> 01:15:47.580
And they block each other.

01:15:47.580 --> 01:15:49.480
There have to be
pathways between them

01:15:49.480 --> 01:15:51.370
so that people can
walk and clean them

01:15:51.370 --> 01:15:53.000
up when they get dirty.

01:15:53.000 --> 01:15:55.750
There's piping and
support structures.

01:15:55.750 --> 01:15:59.840
There are inverters that
change it from DC to AC.

01:15:59.840 --> 01:16:01.900
And that knocks it down
by a factor of about 2

01:16:01.900 --> 01:16:05.850
and 1/2 from 20% to 7 and 1/2%.

01:16:05.850 --> 01:16:09.640
In principle, silicon
can get to about 30%

01:16:09.640 --> 01:16:14.600
efficiency consistent with
the laws of thermodynamics.

01:16:14.600 --> 01:16:16.130
Great.

01:16:16.130 --> 01:16:16.630
Mary?

01:16:19.640 --> 01:16:23.300
Responding to the visionary
nature of the project

01:16:23.300 --> 01:16:28.070
and some of the earlier
works, such as city skies,

01:16:28.070 --> 01:16:31.220
I'm curious if the panel could
comment on whether or not

01:16:31.220 --> 01:16:33.710
they think that it's
possible to even imagine

01:16:33.710 --> 01:16:36.860
physically a floating city.

01:16:36.860 --> 01:16:40.190
I've been very inspired by
and interested in Buckminster

01:16:40.190 --> 01:16:43.460
Fuller's Cloud Nine
project, which is maybe

01:16:43.460 --> 01:16:48.352
a related project, a city
with a mile-wide diameter

01:16:48.352 --> 01:16:49.310
that floats in the sky.

01:16:49.310 --> 01:16:52.970
He makes a very persuasive
argument to a layperson.

01:16:52.970 --> 01:16:55.580
But I'm curious to know if the
panel has any thoughts about

01:16:55.580 --> 01:16:58.120
whether or not this
could be possible.

01:16:58.120 --> 01:16:59.770
Yeah.

01:16:59.770 --> 01:17:00.520
I think so.

01:17:00.520 --> 01:17:02.800
I mean, the problem
is sometimes you--

01:17:05.500 --> 01:17:09.060
something which
is very beautiful,

01:17:09.060 --> 01:17:11.440
if you really take
the challenge yourself

01:17:11.440 --> 01:17:14.035
and you really try to
build it and to build it

01:17:14.035 --> 01:17:16.270
with infrastructure which
is different than the

01:17:16.270 --> 01:17:18.760
infrastructure that
usually things are built

01:17:18.760 --> 01:17:23.420
and with such a responsibly
distributed agency of how

01:17:23.420 --> 01:17:25.840
the project grows bit by bit--

01:17:25.840 --> 01:17:28.180
sometimes I'm very anxious.

01:17:28.180 --> 01:17:30.520
It would be very different
color, very different

01:17:30.520 --> 01:17:33.520
understanding of what it
might be, these cloud cities,

01:17:33.520 --> 01:17:37.112
and which type of
political challenge

01:17:37.112 --> 01:17:38.320
we might be facing with them.

01:17:38.320 --> 01:17:39.760
Now, something very simple.

01:17:39.760 --> 01:17:42.250
When we float and we make a
world-wide record to lift up

01:17:42.250 --> 01:17:45.670
a person in the air with a
certified vehicle in the White

01:17:45.670 --> 01:17:48.310
Sands, when we were
trying to repeat

01:17:48.310 --> 01:17:51.040
the same in a surface
which is not white,

01:17:51.040 --> 01:17:53.590
we were not lifting
even one person.

01:17:53.590 --> 01:17:57.340
This means your power
of how much people

01:17:57.340 --> 01:18:00.190
you can carry up into the
air is really dependent

01:18:00.190 --> 01:18:02.648
about a surface, the albedo,
the reflectivity of the Earth.

01:18:02.648 --> 01:18:04.315
And this means this
could be the clouds,

01:18:04.315 --> 01:18:06.010
or it could be the
surface of the air.

01:18:06.010 --> 01:18:09.520
You can also see and think
about a sea when it's very calm

01:18:09.520 --> 01:18:11.530
and you don't have so
much ripple in the water.

01:18:11.530 --> 01:18:15.500
This means this balloon is
kind of an amazing thermometer.

01:18:15.500 --> 01:18:18.310
It's an amazing sensor,
will go up and down

01:18:18.310 --> 01:18:22.540
and really weave something which
are-- because humans still,

01:18:22.540 --> 01:18:24.460
we are so similar.

01:18:24.460 --> 01:18:26.380
Oh, you cannot
look at the cosmos.

01:18:26.380 --> 01:18:28.690
You're not looking at the--
you're out of the scale,

01:18:28.690 --> 01:18:30.400
is the problems of all on Earth.

01:18:30.400 --> 01:18:32.590
But there are few people
who really understand

01:18:32.590 --> 01:18:36.250
this choreography, this
relationship between the Earth

01:18:36.250 --> 01:18:38.950
and the sun, this idea
what Lodo was doing,

01:18:38.950 --> 01:18:41.080
this drawing, going
up and going down

01:18:41.080 --> 01:18:43.840
and being able to understand
these boundary layers where

01:18:43.840 --> 01:18:44.740
we live.

01:18:44.740 --> 01:18:48.760
And people sometimes are very--
oh, [INAUDIBLE],, you got that,

01:18:48.760 --> 01:18:49.760
or you are getting here.

01:18:49.760 --> 01:18:51.260
You cannot be in between.

01:18:51.260 --> 01:18:53.800
And it's like every
time you try to--

01:18:53.800 --> 01:18:55.840
and this means this
tomorrow is like-- we became

01:18:55.840 --> 01:18:58.090
so much weather dependent.

01:18:58.090 --> 01:18:59.830
Isn't this tomorrow
or maybe not?

01:18:59.830 --> 01:19:01.120
Tomorrow, maybe not.

01:19:01.120 --> 01:19:02.330
And day after tomorrow, not.

01:19:02.330 --> 01:19:04.240
And remember, all the
psychology of people

01:19:04.240 --> 01:19:06.490
is like a turnkey solution.

01:19:06.490 --> 01:19:08.050
It's a cut down.

01:19:08.050 --> 01:19:11.070
Well, here, the cut down
is much more gentle.

01:19:11.070 --> 01:19:13.180
You see the sun coming
from the horizon.

01:19:13.180 --> 01:19:15.010
You kind of float.

01:19:15.010 --> 01:19:17.200
Gravity seems to reverse itself.

01:19:17.200 --> 01:19:19.570
Space is top to be at.

01:19:19.570 --> 01:19:22.030
The current line is
300 kilometers high

01:19:22.030 --> 01:19:24.100
where objects start to fall.

01:19:24.100 --> 01:19:28.150
When we fall upward,
we reverse our senses.

01:19:28.150 --> 01:19:29.080
Stay with the motion.

01:19:29.080 --> 01:19:30.038
You move with the wind.

01:19:30.038 --> 01:19:31.037
You became the wind.

01:19:31.037 --> 01:19:31.870
There are a lot of--

01:19:31.870 --> 01:19:33.820
I mean, if you take
it serious, start to,

01:19:33.820 --> 01:19:36.910
I think so-- in the moment that
a cloud city might be up there,

01:19:36.910 --> 01:19:38.710
I think we might
have solved already

01:19:38.710 --> 01:19:40.185
down here on planet Earth.

01:19:40.185 --> 01:19:41.560
I mean, we have
been learning how

01:19:41.560 --> 01:19:44.530
to live on that bubble
called Earth today.

01:19:44.530 --> 01:19:47.230
And maybe we might get
ready to get another step

01:19:47.230 --> 01:19:49.030
towards somewhere
else, the solar sails

01:19:49.030 --> 01:19:51.368
and wherever it might be there.

01:19:51.368 --> 01:19:51.910
I don't know.

01:19:51.910 --> 01:19:53.920
Another thing, for
example, [INAUDIBLE]

01:19:53.920 --> 01:19:56.080
Institute, kind of the
MIT from Germany, says,

01:19:56.080 --> 01:19:58.390
one might be kind of a
monument that we have built

01:19:58.390 --> 01:20:00.520
Eiffel Tower, the London Eye.

01:20:00.520 --> 01:20:03.530
Think about a city again
because usually, what we do

01:20:03.530 --> 01:20:04.480
is educational.

01:20:04.480 --> 01:20:06.310
When it's a high
wind voltage, you

01:20:06.310 --> 01:20:08.980
have a lot of problem
to storage in batteries

01:20:08.980 --> 01:20:10.330
and the distribution, also.

01:20:10.330 --> 01:20:11.380
And this means the
best things you

01:20:11.380 --> 01:20:12.838
can do when you
have a lot of wind,

01:20:12.838 --> 01:20:15.070
you get an SMS in your
telephone telling you,

01:20:15.070 --> 01:20:16.990
now wash your
clothes because it's

01:20:16.990 --> 01:20:18.898
cheaper at the high
rate of-- that's

01:20:18.898 --> 01:20:20.440
the moment you have
to consume energy

01:20:20.440 --> 01:20:21.370
because there is a lot of wind.

01:20:21.370 --> 01:20:23.230
And this means we're
thinking like, OK,

01:20:23.230 --> 01:20:25.570
think about a mini cloud city.

01:20:25.570 --> 01:20:28.070
Something when it's very windy,
usually, it will be down.

01:20:28.070 --> 01:20:30.550
This means it's the time
to wash your clothes.

01:20:30.550 --> 01:20:33.340
This means the Eiffel Tower
could fly up and down.

01:20:33.340 --> 01:20:36.640
And it's a symbol for the
city of reconnecting yourself

01:20:36.640 --> 01:20:38.350
with something
that sometimes you

01:20:38.350 --> 01:20:40.960
can just pick up the clothes
and put it under the sun to dry.

01:20:40.960 --> 01:20:43.580
It's very tortuous,
this addiction

01:20:43.580 --> 01:20:45.500
we have to certain relation.

01:20:45.500 --> 01:20:49.690
So I actually want to dive
into this question myself.

01:20:49.690 --> 01:20:52.630
So I run a group called
the Urban Metabolism Group.

01:20:52.630 --> 01:20:56.080
And what we do is
account for the resources

01:20:56.080 --> 01:20:58.910
that we all consume
on an annual basis.

01:20:58.910 --> 01:21:04.570
So are floating cloud
cities possible?

01:21:04.570 --> 01:21:09.250
Well, so all of you on average
in this part of the world

01:21:09.250 --> 01:21:14.630
consume between 25 and 30
tons per capita per year.

01:21:14.630 --> 01:21:18.490
So if we can figure out a way
to put 20 to 30 tons per capita,

01:21:18.490 --> 01:21:22.270
each one of us up in the
sky, then it's possible, yes.

01:21:22.270 --> 01:21:24.880
The other answer I want to
give, the other the second part

01:21:24.880 --> 01:21:29.610
to this answer is there is
a city of 8 million people

01:21:29.610 --> 01:21:32.640
in the sky every day, in fact.

01:21:32.640 --> 01:21:35.730
That's the average number
of people who fly every day.

01:21:35.730 --> 01:21:39.400
And they're connected on
their iPads and everything.

01:21:39.400 --> 01:21:42.570
So there is a city
in the sky today.

01:21:42.570 --> 01:21:45.160
It's fossil fuel driven.

01:21:45.160 --> 01:21:47.070
It's not that vision.

01:21:47.070 --> 01:21:48.630
But it's absolutely there.

01:21:48.630 --> 01:21:53.980
So that's really very
much our reality.

01:21:53.980 --> 01:21:55.270
Other questions?

01:21:55.270 --> 01:21:59.240
Please go to the mic if you can.

01:21:59.240 --> 01:22:02.925
The question is more
about [INAUDIBLE]

01:22:02.925 --> 01:22:07.280
you say about having that
city floating, by example.

01:22:07.280 --> 01:22:10.100
80 years ago, just
thinking about flying,

01:22:10.100 --> 01:22:12.380
just one person flying
by itself, right?

01:22:12.380 --> 01:22:14.750
Now you can have 380 floating.

01:22:14.750 --> 01:22:15.680
And he's right.

01:22:15.680 --> 01:22:19.310
It's almost a city floating
there, just with an engine.

01:22:19.310 --> 01:22:21.948
But when you really make
your imagination go,

01:22:21.948 --> 01:22:23.490
it's feasible that
you start floating

01:22:23.490 --> 01:22:25.850
there or the helium balloons.

01:22:25.850 --> 01:22:27.820
So that's quite feasible.

01:22:27.820 --> 01:22:31.520
Now, my question here is
there's great minds here trying

01:22:31.520 --> 01:22:32.480
to do the right thing.

01:22:32.480 --> 01:22:34.943
But then you have the other
side, the economy side.

01:22:34.943 --> 01:22:36.110
And they say, you know what?

01:22:36.110 --> 01:22:38.300
We are the owners of
the oil companies.

01:22:38.300 --> 01:22:40.190
We are the owners of
the oil companies.

01:22:40.190 --> 01:22:43.550
We really don't want to fund
you because if we fund you,

01:22:43.550 --> 01:22:45.350
we're going to be useless.

01:22:45.350 --> 01:22:49.080
How we deal with that politics
side and say, you know what?

01:22:49.080 --> 01:22:50.942
How do we convince
the people that

01:22:50.942 --> 01:22:53.150
has the funding to actually
invest in the right thing

01:22:53.150 --> 01:22:53.803
to do?

01:22:53.803 --> 01:22:55.220
I don't want to
fund you because I

01:22:55.220 --> 01:22:56.450
want to keep sustaining myself.

01:22:56.450 --> 01:22:57.500
I don't care about
what's happening

01:22:57.500 --> 01:22:58.790
with the rest of that.

01:22:58.790 --> 01:23:01.460
Who would like to tackle that?

01:23:01.460 --> 01:23:02.180
Come on Friday.

01:23:02.180 --> 01:23:04.010
Come tomorrow and
day after tomorrow.

01:23:04.010 --> 01:23:04.820
We keep talking.

01:23:04.820 --> 01:23:06.730
You know what I mean?

01:23:06.730 --> 01:23:07.580
We might get it.

01:23:07.580 --> 01:23:08.960
It's interesting, these panels.

01:23:08.960 --> 01:23:12.290
It strikes me that this
could be a day-long symposium

01:23:12.290 --> 01:23:14.870
because there are some major
themes that are just going

01:23:14.870 --> 01:23:16.610
to get not really touched on.

01:23:16.610 --> 01:23:18.720
But this is obviously
one of them.

01:23:18.720 --> 01:23:21.530
I guess I could just
say a word on it,

01:23:21.530 --> 01:23:25.280
maybe taking it one step
further, which is that it's

01:23:25.280 --> 01:23:26.360
very hard for us to say.

01:23:26.360 --> 01:23:29.630
We live here in a developed
country that developed itself

01:23:29.630 --> 01:23:31.680
on a carbon economy, right?

01:23:31.680 --> 01:23:33.530
I mean, so we have
the quality of life

01:23:33.530 --> 01:23:37.130
that we have because
of fossil fuels here.

01:23:37.130 --> 01:23:39.030
Everything around us
was built on that.

01:23:39.030 --> 01:23:42.260
And so one of the problems
is, moving forward,

01:23:42.260 --> 01:23:45.020
not just keeping things the same
but acknowledging that there's

01:23:45.020 --> 01:23:47.250
developing countries.

01:23:47.250 --> 01:23:50.210
And this is like saying
to them, you can't develop

01:23:50.210 --> 01:23:51.980
in the same way
that we did, but not

01:23:51.980 --> 01:23:56.227
at the same time offering
them a replacement technology.

01:23:56.227 --> 01:23:58.310
So I guess I'm just taking
your question maybe one

01:23:58.310 --> 01:23:59.360
step further.

01:23:59.360 --> 01:24:03.710
But I firmly believe that
one of the things that we

01:24:03.710 --> 01:24:05.470
are going to have
to do as a society

01:24:05.470 --> 01:24:07.250
is make the hard
choices coming up.

01:24:07.250 --> 01:24:11.240
And I think it was pointed out
that we've already maybe gotten

01:24:11.240 --> 01:24:15.860
to this point where we see the
onset of severe storms, higher

01:24:15.860 --> 01:24:18.530
temperatures, heat
deaths, things like that.

01:24:18.530 --> 01:24:20.030
At some point, we're
not going to be

01:24:20.030 --> 01:24:21.360
able to ignore it anymore.

01:24:21.360 --> 01:24:23.420
And because a few
corporations wish

01:24:23.420 --> 01:24:26.000
to continue to
make a profit, it's

01:24:26.000 --> 01:24:29.510
not going to balance out
with the increasing penalties

01:24:29.510 --> 01:24:31.520
that we're all paying
as a civilization,

01:24:31.520 --> 01:24:33.320
as a society for those things.

01:24:33.320 --> 01:24:34.820
And it's going to
be a slow process.

01:24:34.820 --> 01:24:38.870
I don't think that-- some
people think about turning

01:24:38.870 --> 01:24:40.610
fossil fuels off tomorrow.

01:24:40.610 --> 01:24:41.760
That's not going to happen.

01:24:41.760 --> 01:24:44.150
It's going to be
a gradual decline.

01:24:44.150 --> 01:24:47.390
Some of those power
plants you were showing

01:24:47.390 --> 01:24:50.960
were being put online now, and
they have lifetimes of decades.

01:24:50.960 --> 01:24:52.658
Those aren't going
to be shut off.

01:24:52.658 --> 01:24:54.200
The ones that are
coming online today

01:24:54.200 --> 01:24:56.930
are going to be around as long
as I'm going to be around.

01:24:56.930 --> 01:24:58.820
But what we can
hope is that there's

01:24:58.820 --> 01:25:00.710
going to be more of
the renewables, maybe

01:25:00.710 --> 01:25:04.190
some of the other alternative
energy technologies coming

01:25:04.190 --> 01:25:08.070
into play, and that we slowly
ramp those things down.

01:25:08.070 --> 01:25:10.160
But I do have to bring
us back to this idea

01:25:10.160 --> 01:25:12.560
that we do have to think
about developing countries

01:25:12.560 --> 01:25:15.590
at the same time, handing
technology off to them

01:25:15.590 --> 01:25:18.650
and trying to see them
develop not in our footsteps,

01:25:18.650 --> 01:25:20.870
not with the carbon
footprint that we've

01:25:20.870 --> 01:25:23.570
had, but with a new
footprint, hopefully much more

01:25:23.570 --> 01:25:26.112
renewable, hopefully much more
of these alternative energies.

01:25:26.112 --> 01:25:27.195
So sorry if I interrupted.

01:25:27.195 --> 01:25:27.830
We have to be--

01:25:27.830 --> 01:25:29.312
No, not at all.

01:25:29.312 --> 01:25:31.020
Well, I wanted to say
a couple of things.

01:25:31.020 --> 01:25:34.700
One is that I think
it's a delusion

01:25:34.700 --> 01:25:36.260
to think that the
laws of physics

01:25:36.260 --> 01:25:42.110
are going to be surmounted
in the next era.

01:25:42.110 --> 01:25:44.442
100 years ago, people
didn't know how to fly.

01:25:44.442 --> 01:25:45.650
And now they know how to fly.

01:25:45.650 --> 01:25:46.160
That's true.

01:25:46.160 --> 01:25:48.380
But the laws of buoyancy
have been understood

01:25:48.380 --> 01:25:51.590
for a long time, as have
the laws of absorption

01:25:51.590 --> 01:25:53.730
and the emission of radiation.

01:25:53.730 --> 01:25:56.960
And there's no sign
that there's going

01:25:56.960 --> 01:25:59.540
to be a change in those
laws over the lifetime

01:25:59.540 --> 01:26:02.120
that we're concerned with.

01:26:02.120 --> 01:26:05.360
The other thing I wanted to
mention is I really want to say

01:26:05.360 --> 01:26:09.980
a word in favor of the kind of
art that Tomás is involved in.

01:26:09.980 --> 01:26:12.050
I think art has a
political impact

01:26:12.050 --> 01:26:15.350
and can be used to
agitate and change

01:26:15.350 --> 01:26:17.030
people's minds about things.

01:26:17.030 --> 01:26:23.040
And when you talk about
influencing companies,

01:26:23.040 --> 01:26:26.190
if you create a movement, if
you create political agitation,

01:26:26.190 --> 01:26:28.950
if you elect people
who will pass a carbon

01:26:28.950 --> 01:26:34.560
tax to place a cost on the
externalities of filling up

01:26:34.560 --> 01:26:37.560
the atmosphere
with a toxic gas, I

01:26:37.560 --> 01:26:39.150
think you'll have
the possibility

01:26:39.150 --> 01:26:40.140
of making real changes.

01:26:42.730 --> 01:26:43.240
Yup.

01:26:43.240 --> 01:26:45.790
The other, which I want to add,
we have to be very careful.

01:26:45.790 --> 01:26:48.340
And maybe Caroline
can help me and Leila

01:26:48.340 --> 01:26:49.900
on the idea of the we, we, we.

01:26:49.900 --> 01:26:53.260
No, there are many people that
hate the term "Anthropocene"

01:26:53.260 --> 01:26:56.170
because actually, who produces
these mess where we are living

01:26:56.170 --> 01:26:58.300
is a small percentage
of a population who

01:26:58.300 --> 01:26:59.470
live on planet Earth.

01:26:59.470 --> 01:27:02.087
And then many people call
it a Capitalocene, right?

01:27:02.087 --> 01:27:03.670
This means we have
to be very careful.

01:27:03.670 --> 01:27:05.628
Because all of a sudden,
it's like, we, we, we.

01:27:05.628 --> 01:27:07.180
Well, actually,
there are many people

01:27:07.180 --> 01:27:10.930
who know how to live and
relate on planet Earth actually

01:27:10.930 --> 01:27:13.310
causing the problem that then
later are putting in danger

01:27:13.310 --> 01:27:15.280
the people who are
suffering the consequences

01:27:15.280 --> 01:27:18.660
of hyperconsumptions.

01:27:18.660 --> 01:27:20.000
It's really imbalanced.

01:27:20.000 --> 01:27:22.780
And then somehow we always
keep calling "we humans," where

01:27:22.780 --> 01:27:24.370
we put everybody in the bag.

01:27:24.370 --> 01:27:26.960
It's quite a complicated
issue over that.

01:27:29.500 --> 01:27:33.230
So we're at the end
of the panel session.

01:27:33.230 --> 01:27:34.840
Thank you so much for coming.

01:27:34.840 --> 01:27:36.760
But please join me in
thanking the panel.

01:27:36.760 --> 01:27:40.141
[APPLAUSE]

01:27:44.500 --> 01:27:48.250
And especially Tomás and
Joaquin and the whole team.

01:27:48.250 --> 01:27:54.640
Please join us in Lobby 10
for the Aerocene demonstration

01:27:54.640 --> 01:27:56.050
of the flow predictor.

01:27:56.050 --> 01:28:00.580
And weather permitting,
tomorrow and Saturday,

01:28:00.580 --> 01:28:05.320
please join us in Killian Court
for floating the Aerocene.

01:28:05.320 --> 01:28:07.140
Thank you.

